('9a5d75c2-ed08-54d2-afb7-302d753f2e46'::uuid,'28d81dcf-4bf7-51ee-8a3f-ed571ec8d617'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-D. EOC PERSONNEL DESIGNATION REGISTER\n\n| **EOC role**             | **Participating office** | **Primary personnel** | **Alternate** | **Relevant training / competence** | **Designation date** | **Availability / notes** |\n|--------------------------|--------------------------|-----------------------|---------------|------------------------------------|----------------------|--------------------------|\n| EOC Manager              |                          |                       |               |                                    |                      |                          |\n| Operations               |                          |                       |               |                                    |                      |                          |\n| Planning / Information   |                          |                       |               |                                    |                      |                          |\n| Logistics                |                          |                       |               |                                    |                      |                          |\n| Administration / Finance |                          |                       |               |                                    |                      |                          |\n| Agency / cluster liaison |                          |                       |               |                                    |                      |                          |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-D.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('a6a635cc-852a-55bd-8708-5ebcc93cc3bf'::uuid,'4c27e9a2-75d0-5ab4-8d30-de80e669aab0'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-E. DUTY HANDOVER CHECKLIST\n\n- Current hazard bulletins, warnings and validity periods.\n\n- Active incidents, affected locations and current operational status.\n\n- Pending decisions, approvals, requests and deadlines.\n\n- Resource requests, deployment status and unresolved constraints.\n\n- Communications, ICT, facility and equipment status.\n\n- Personnel availability, welfare concerns and relief arrangements.\n\n- Records, logs, reports and attachments completed or pending.\n\n- Escalation items and person / office responsible for follow-up.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-E.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('38cb4591-f60c-55c6-b7d0-3c9250561cc4'::uuid,'8f35192b-ad03-56b9-904a-60cb2594d593'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-F. LEGAL AND TECHNICAL VALIDATION REGISTER\n\n| **Validation issue**                                      | **Required reviewer**               | **Validation required before approval**                                                             |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Current legal establishment and approved CDRRMO structure | City Legal Office; HRMO; CDRRMO     | Identify existing ordinance/EO, approved organogram and any required amendatory issuance.           |\n| JMC No. 2014-1 requirements and current applicability     | HRMO; CDRRMO; OCD Region VIII       | Verify exact staffing, division and qualification requirements and any superseding guidance.        |\n| Plantilla, position classification and funding            | HRMO; Budget; DBM as applicable     | Confirm authorized items, salary grades, filled/vacant status and personnel-services affordability. |\n| Qualification standards and appointment status            | HRMO; CSC as applicable             | Verify current qualification standards and appointment records.                                     |\n| EOC staffing guidance and B4 requirements                 | OCD Region VIII; CDRRMO             | Confirm current EOC guidelines, role structure, alternates and liaison requirements.                |\n| 24/7 duty and compensation arrangements                   | HRMO; Budget; CLO                   | Verify duty, overtime, rest, hazard pay and other entitlements under applicable rules.              |\n| Insurance, PPE and psychosocial support                   | HRMO; CHO; OSH focal office; Budget | Verify available coverage, policy, funding, referral and incident-reporting procedures.             |\n| Privacy and personnel records                             | DPO; HRMO; Records Officer; ICT     | Confirm lawful basis, access, retention, security and disclosure controls.                          |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-F.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('42fbe119-3192-52a2-817f-505e85d935a7'::uuid,'85e5170e-7cb8-57d6-95c2-bd35b6f46fe5'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-G \\| SOP HR-01 – ANNUAL WORKFORCE PLANNING AND PLANTILLA REVIEW\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure the CDRRMO has a lawful, risk-informed and adequately planned workforce without creating unauthorized posts or commitments.\n\n**Trigger**: Annual planning cycle; material change in mandate, workload, risk profile, staffing or assessment results.\n\n**Owner**: CDRRMO Head jointly with HRMO for workforce-planning controls.\n\n**Procedure:**\n\n1\\. Division leads submit workload, service demand, outputs, critical functions and staffing availability.\n\n2\\. CDRRMO consolidates workload against LDRRMP priorities, statutory functions, operating hours and continuity requirements.\n\n3\\. HRMO certifies approved structure, plantilla, filled/vacant items, qualification standards and personnel status.\n\n4\\. Identify gaps by category: authorized vacancy, skills gap, workload imbalance, process inefficiency, equipment constraint or inter-office dependency.\n\n5\\. Develop options: internal redeployment within authority, training, process redesign, lawful temporary augmentation, or formal position/structure proposal.\n\n6\\. Budget assesses affordability and recurring costs; CLO reviews legal route; other offices review relevant impacts.\n\n7\\. Submit proposals to the competent approving authority. Do not implement unapproved structural or fiscal changes.\n\n8\\. Record approved actions, owners, deadlines and evidence; review quarterly and annually.\n\nRecords: annual workforce plan; certified plantilla; gap matrix; workload analysis; endorsements; approval; implementation and CAPA register.\n\nEscalation: unresolved critical vacancies or unsafe coverage are elevated to the Mayor through established administrative channels, with interim risk controls documented.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-G.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('8322bd13-4168-5416-8b83-efa12467cd91'::uuid,'0c47ee75-7e9d-5306-9fb1-3a56ad06e956'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-H \\| SOP HR-02 – POSITION DESCRIPTION AND COMPETENCY PROFILE CONTROL\n\n**Purpose**: Keep every authorized position''s duties, outputs, authority boundaries and competency requirements current and aligned with statutory CDRRMO functions.\n\n**Owner**: HRMO for official position-description control; CDRRMO Head for technical content.\n\n**Procedure**:\n\n1\\. Identify new, revised or materially changed function.\n\n2\\. Division lead drafts role purpose, outputs, duties, reporting line, decision limits, interfaces, working conditions and competency profile.\n\n3\\. CDRRMO Head verifies alignment with RA 10121 and approved functional architecture.\n\n4\\. HRMO checks consistency with qualification standards, position classification and CSC requirements.\n\n5\\. CLO reviews legal implications where authority or delegation is affected.\n\n6\\. Obtain required approval; assign version and effective date.\n\n7\\. Communicate role and performance expectations to incumbent and supervisor.\n\n8\\. Review annually and after organizational, legal or operational change.\n\nRecords: approved PDF, competency profile, consultation record, approval and revision history.\n\nControl: a job description cannot expand statutory authority or substitute for a valid delegation.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-H.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('a4be90ec-234e-5701-87b0-b268dc16b432'::uuid,'844f110f-9691-5b15-ae90-e8d3a8a71bff'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-I \\| SOP HR-03 – RECRUITMENT, APPOINTMENT INTERFACE AND ONBOARDING\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure staffing actions are based on authorized positions and comply with civil-service and City HR rules.\n\n**Owner**: HRMO; CDRRMO provides technical role requirements and induction.\n\n**Procedure**:\n\n1\\. HRMO confirms an authorized vacant plantilla item and applicable qualification standards.\n\n2\\. CDRRMO validates technical competencies and operational requirements without adding unauthorized eligibility criteria.\n\n3\\. HRMO processes publication, assessment, selection and documentation under the approved Merit Selection Plan and applicable CSC rules.\n\n4\\. Selection/recommendation is documented; appointing authority exercises appointment power.\n\n5\\. HRMO completes appointment, assumption and personnel-record requirements.\n\n6\\. CDRRMO supervisor conducts induction on mandate, MOO, role boundaries, safety, information security, privacy, records and coordination protocols.\n\n7\\. Agree initial outputs, supervision and learning needs; record completion.\n\n8\\. Review onboarding effectiveness and update induction materials.\n\nRecords: vacancy certification; approved PDF/QS; recruitment records; appointment; assumption; induction checklist; initial performance plan.\n\nControl: CDRRMO may not promise appointment, bypass HRMO or deploy an appointee before lawful assumption and required clearances.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-I.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('a42b6d32-1b96-5ad9-b938-ba416203e64d'::uuid,'dafc267f-f2be-51fb-99e4-c3590777ffea'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-J \\| SOP HR-04 – COMPETENCY ASSESSMENT AND LEARNING & DEVELOPMENT\n\n**Purpose**: Build demonstrable capability for risk reduction, preparedness, coordination and safe performance.\n\n**Owner**: CDRRMO Head; HRMO validates agency L&D and competency-based HR alignment.\n\n**Procedure**:\n\n1\\. Extract competencies from approved position profiles and operational task requirements.\n\n2\\. Assess current capability using records, structured assessment, observed performance, exercises and supervisor validation.\n\n3\\. Identify proficiency gaps and prioritize by life-safety, legal compliance, criticality and likelihood of deployment.\n\n4\\. Prepare an annual L&D plan with objectives, target participants, prerequisites, provider, budget, schedule and evaluation method.\n\n5\\. Obtain HRMO and budget review and required approvals.\n\n6\\. Deliver training, coaching, mentoring, drills or supervised practice.\n\n7\\. Evaluate learning and demonstrated competence; attendance or certificates alone are insufficient for high-risk task authorization.\n\n8\\. Update competency records and duty eligibility; schedule refresher or remediation.\n\n9\\. Review outcomes and revise the plan using exercise and incident lessons.\n\nRecords: competency matrix; TNA; approved L&D plan; attendance; assessment; certificates; exercise evaluation; authorization/qualification register.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-J.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('a0de96e3-9c65-5d6c-85ef-0010eb9c4805'::uuid,'bd06e310-098b-5af3-91db-49f19c207902'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-K \\| SOP HR-05 – DUTY ROSTER, SHIFT HANDOVER AND PERSONNEL AVAILABILITY\n\n**Purpose**: Maintain reliable personnel coverage and safe transfer of responsibility during normal operations and heightened readiness.\n\n**Owner**: CDRRMO Head; division leads prepare and supervise rosters.\n\n**Procedure**:\n\n1\\. Determine required coverage from approved service hours, hazard season, duty requirements and continuity plan.\n\n2\\. Confirm personnel availability, qualifications, alternates, leave, rest periods and constraints.\n\n3\\. Prepare roster and submit for approval under delegated authority.\n\n4\\. Publish roster securely and notify assigned personnel.\n\n5\\. Outgoing officers brief incoming officer on open tasks, warnings, incidents, contacts, equipment status, pending approvals and risks.\n\n6\\. Both officers acknowledge handover; record time, unresolved issues and escalation.\n\n7\\. Immediately report absence, unfilled post, unsafe fatigue or inability to cover a critical function to the supervisor.\n\n8\\. Arrange authorized replacement and record changes.\n\n9\\. Reconcile actual coverage, attendance and exceptions monthly; feed gaps to CAPA.\n\nRecords: duty roster; acknowledgement; attendance; handover checklist; exception and fatigue log.\n\nBoundary: this SOP does not define EOC activation level, incident command or operational period; refer to the designated EOC/ICS chapters.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-K.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('eb3df260-29a4-53dd-8bb4-07c67303e847'::uuid,'0ce9bff4-b53d-55fc-b926-932384187d3f'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-L \\| SOP HR-06 – EOC PERSONNEL DESIGNATION AND SURGE AUGMENTATION\n\n**Purpose**: Ensure personnel assigned to EOC functions are designated, available, competent and supported by their parent offices.\n\n**Owner**: CDRRMO Head for roster readiness; EOC Manager for activated EOC staffing coordination; HRMO for personnel administration.\n\n**Procedure**:\n\n1\\. Identify EOC functions and minimum competencies from the approved EOC structure and job action sheets in the relevant chapters.\n\n2\\. Request nominations from City offices and partner agencies, including primary and alternate personnel.\n\n3\\. Verify role fit, training, contactability, availability, supervisor concurrence and conflict/continuity implications.\n\n4\\. Compile a controlled designation register; obtain approval by the authority specified in the EOC issuance.\n\n5\\. Conduct orientation and exercises; record gaps and refresher needs.\n\n6\\. On activation, EOC Manager requests staffing through approved activation procedures; parent offices release or substitute personnel consistent with authority and service continuity.\n\n7\\. Track attendance, shift rotation, welfare, access permissions and return-to-office arrangements.\n\n8\\. Deactivate or revise assignments through authorized procedures; reconcile records and lessons.\n\nRecords: designation instrument; roster; alternates; competency evidence; exercise records; release/return log.\n\nControl: designation to an EOC position does not transfer the person''s statutory agency mandate or create field command authority.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-L.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('9722d1b3-3f7e-5e29-afd4-45ab97cc558a'::uuid,'bda079cf-0360-5c94-b2ef-f65c245f062b'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-M \\| SOP HR-07 – PERSONNEL SAFETY, WELFARE, INJURY AND PSYCHOSOCIAL REFERRAL\n\n**Purpose**: Prevent harm and ensure prompt support for CDRRMO personnel and responders under the City''s responsibility.\n\n**Owner**: CDRRMO Head for implementation; HRMO, designated OSH personnel and health/MHPSS providers for their respective professional mandates.\n\n**Procedure**:\n\n1\\. Identify task hazards and required controls before assignment; verify PPE, training, fitness and supervision.\n\n2\\. Brief personnel on hazards, stop-work authority, communications, emergency contacts and welfare arrangements.\n\n3\\. Monitor exposure, fatigue, hydration, rest, heat, psychosocial stress and changing conditions.\n\n4\\. If injury, exposure, unsafe condition or distress occurs, prioritize immediate safety, medical care and protective action.\n\n5\\. Notify supervisor and designated safety focal; complete incident report as soon as practicable without delaying care.\n\n6\\. Refer to appropriate medical, occupational health, insurance, HR or psychosocial service with consent and confidentiality safeguards.\n\n7\\. Assess return-to-duty, accommodation and restrictions through competent professionals and HRMO processes.\n\n8\\. Review causes and contributing system factors; implement corrective actions and verify effectiveness.\n\nRecords: task risk assessment; PPE issue register; briefing; incident/near-miss report; referral record with restricted access; corrective action and return-to-duty clearance.\n\nSafeguards: protect sensitive personal information; do not disclose diagnosis or counselling details in operational logs; comply with applicable OSH, privacy and mental health requirements.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-M.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('fc1052f3-2651-5b90-ad1c-b4e2f9c07c6b'::uuid,'a1781689-7c69-5c11-b6d1-f99201d66c85'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-N \\| SOP HR-08 – PERFORMANCE MANAGEMENT, RECORDS AND CORRECTIVE ACTION\n\n**Purpose**: Align individual performance with CDRRMO outputs and use evidence to improve capability and institutional processes.\n\n**Owner**: CDRRMO Head and supervisors for operational performance; HRMO for official SPMS and personnel record administration.\n\n**Procedure**:\n\n1\\. Cascade approved office targets into individual outputs consistent with official SPMS.\n\n2\\. Agree measurable deliverables, indicators, timelines and evidence with each staff member.\n\n3\\. Conduct periodic coaching and retain appropriate work evidence.\n\n4\\. Assess results fairly against approved standards and consider workload, resources, dependencies and system constraints.\n\n5\\. Identify whether the gap is individual, supervisory, procedural, resource-related or inter-agency.\n\n6\\. Agree support and corrective action; refer possible misconduct or administrative matters through HRMO and due process.\n\n7\\. Verify completion and effectiveness; recognize good performance under approved policies.\n\n8\\. Aggregate systemic issues for workforce planning, risk management and MOO revision.\n\nRecords: approved performance commitments; coaching records; evidence; improvement plan; CAPA; review and acknowledgement.\n\nControl: this SOP does not create a separate disciplinary process or override CSC rules, due process or the City''s SPMS.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-N.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('8cd803c0-7c3d-54ec-85da-0cf84f643044'::uuid,'9cd3b25d-8b71-5b03-8180-37534b5a11e3'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-O \\| WORKFORCE AND PLANTILLA REVIEW FORM (HR-F01)\n\n**Purpose**: Standardized record for the process identified in the title. Complete only fields necessary for the function; maintain restricted personal or sensitive information separately with access controls.\n\n| Field                                                    | Required entry |\n|----------------------------------------------------------|----------------|\n| Planning year / review date                              |                |\n| Division / functional area                               |                |\n| Approved organizational unit / issuance                  |                |\n| Authorized plantilla item / position title / item number |                |\n| Authorized / filled / vacant / acting / temporary status |                |\n| Required qualification standard and competency profile   |                |\n| Current workload, outputs and service coverage           |                |\n| Vacancy / capability / workload / continuity risk        |                |\n| Evidence reference and verification date                 |                |\n| Options assessed and rationale                           |                |\n| HRMO review / certification                              |                |\n| Budget review and recurring cost estimate                |                |\n| CLO review where required                                |                |\n| Approving authority / decision / date                    |                |\n| Action owner / due date / closure evidence               |                |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-O.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('0bb86123-0a8f-55f6-9937-5f952c5da34f'::uuid,'a4cfafbe-4b4c-5421-8684-628260e3bcc9'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-P \\| POSITION DESCRIPTION AND COMPETENCY PROFILE TEMPLATE (HR-F02)\n\n**Purpose**: Standardized record for the process identified in the title. Complete only fields necessary for the function; maintain restricted personal or sensitive information separately with access controls.\n\n| Field                                                         | Required entry |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------|----------------|\n| Position title / item number / salary grade (as authorized)   |                |\n| Office / division / immediate supervisor                      |                |\n| Position purpose and contribution to RA 10121 functions       |                |\n| Major duties and measurable outputs                           |                |\n| Decision authority and limits / delegated authority reference |                |\n| Internal and external interfaces                              |                |\n| Minimum qualification standards verified by HRMO              |                |\n| Core, leadership and technical competencies                   |                |\n| Proficiency level and observable behavioural indicators       |                |\n| Work environment / hazards / PPE / duty requirements          |                |\n| Performance indicators and evidence sources                   |                |\n| Prepared by / reviewed by HRMO / approved by authority        |                |\n| Version / effective date / review date                        |                |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-P.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('593e5e0a-b23b-5d70-bb75-4e5f80d67069'::uuid,'bd2bda56-0e37-5910-ba8d-2107a7590b33'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-Q \\| TRAINING NEEDS AND COMPETENCY ASSESSMENT (HR-F03)\n\n**Purpose**: Standardized record for the process identified in the title. Complete only fields necessary for the function; maintain restricted personal or sensitive information separately with access controls.\n\n| Field                                                       | Required entry |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------|----------------|\n| Position / incumbent / assessor / assessment date           |                |\n| Competency / task / proficiency required                    |                |\n| Current proficiency and evidence                            |                |\n| Gap and operational risk if unaddressed                     |                |\n| Priority / criticality                                      |                |\n| Learning intervention / prerequisite                        |                |\n| Provider / schedule / estimated cost                        |                |\n| Assessment method (test, observation, exercise, simulation) |                |\n| Demonstrated competence / authorization decision            |                |\n| Refresher due date / reassessment result                    |                |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-Q.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('df243a74-e48b-5b35-9d39-b4f7b7f23ca2'::uuid,'3fb35d44-6b35-5977-80d1-49a0037adc37'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-R \\| DUTY ROSTER AND HANDOVER FORM (HR-F04)\n\n**Purpose**: Standardized record for the process identified in the title. Complete only fields necessary for the function; maintain restricted personal or sensitive information separately with access controls.\n\n| Field                                                                | Required entry |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------|\n| Duty period / shift / function                                       |                |\n| Primary officer / alternate / contact                                |                |\n| Required competency and qualification verified                       |                |\n| Supervisor approval / date                                           |                |\n| Outgoing officer: open tasks, warnings, incidents, pending decisions |                |\n| Equipment / communications / access status                           |                |\n| Incoming officer acknowledgement / time                              |                |\n| Unresolved risk / escalation / assigned owner                        |                |\n| Fatigue / rest / welfare concern and action                          |                |\n| Exception / replacement / approval reference                         |                |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-R.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('0d107f75-b92c-5f70-9736-0a5b7189eb99'::uuid,'51870f29-6811-5c03-8d0b-1df106573bc8'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-S \\| EOC PERSONNEL DESIGNATION REGISTER (HR-F05)\n\n**Purpose**: Standardized record for the process identified in the title. Complete only fields necessary for the function; maintain restricted personal or sensitive information separately with access controls.\n\n| Field                                                | Required entry |\n|------------------------------------------------------|----------------|\n| EOC function / position / job action sheet reference |                |\n| Parent office / agency                               |                |\n| Primary nominee / alternate                          |                |\n| Supervisor concurrence / designation authority       |                |\n| Relevant training / competency evidence              |                |\n| Contact and availability verification date           |                |\n| Access / information classification requirements     |                |\n| Exercise participation / performance observations    |                |\n| Activation attendance / shift history                |                |\n| Release / return-to-duty / review date               |                |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-S.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('be199b4c-5413-581a-a1c7-e03bdd880f33'::uuid,'957e7bed-18fa-529e-b5d8-e38b08b21f65'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-T \\| PERSONNEL SAFETY, INJURY AND WELFARE REPORT (HR-F06)\n\n**Purpose**: Standardized record for the process identified in the title. Complete only fields necessary for the function; maintain restricted personal or sensitive information separately with access controls.\n\n| Field                                                                      | Required entry |\n|----------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------|\n| Incident / near-miss reference; date/time/location                         |                |\n| Task / activity / supervisor                                               |                |\n| Hazard / exposure / event description (factual)                            |                |\n| Immediate protective action and medical assistance                         |                |\n| PPE / training / briefing / supervision status                             |                |\n| Persons notified and time                                                  |                |\n| Referral made (restricted details stored separately)                       |                |\n| Work restriction / accommodation / fitness decision by competent authority |                |\n| Root/contributing factors and corrective action                            |                |\n| Verification / effectiveness / closure date                                |                |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-T.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('056851be-bfeb-54bc-814a-3f08aa62fd1d'::uuid,'b6874943-8b79-5a4f-b897-36d98f054ea6'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-U \\| CHAPTER 2 CAPA AND QUALITY REVIEW REGISTER (HR-F07)\n\n**Purpose**: Standardized record for the process identified in the title. Complete only fields necessary for the function; maintain restricted personal or sensitive information separately with access controls.\n\n| Field                                                         | Required entry |\n|---------------------------------------------------------------|----------------|\n| Finding source / indicator reference (e.g., A2.1–A2.7)        |                |\n| Nonconformity / gap and evidence                              |                |\n| Risk / consequence / priority                                 |                |\n| Root cause (system, process, competency, resource, interface) |                |\n| Corrective action / expected result                           |                |\n| Accountable owner / responsible actors                        |                |\n| Resources / approval dependency / due date                    |                |\n| Implementation evidence                                       |                |\n| Effectiveness test / verifier / review date                   |                |\n| Status / escalation / closure authorization                   |                |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-U.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('56c71f18-974c-5cbb-8dc0-d595c36f9c1f'::uuid,'8016a091-d567-5dcd-89a2-19624f79f243'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-V \\| CHAPTER 2 RECORDS AND CONTROLLED DOCUMENT REGISTER (HR-F08)\n\n**Purpose**: Standardized record for the process identified in the title. Complete only fields necessary for the function; maintain restricted personal or sensitive information separately with access controls.\n\n| Field                                                   | Required entry |\n|---------------------------------------------------------|----------------|\n| Record / document title and code                        |                |\n| Owner / custodian / system of record                    |                |\n| Version / approval / effective date                     |                |\n| Classification / personal data category                 |                |\n| Authorized access / sharing basis                       |                |\n| Retention and disposition authority                     |                |\n| Backup / security / recovery control                    |                |\n| Review date / superseded version / disposition evidence |                |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-V.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft'),
('d6a1d908-9e65-51d2-995b-177b4159c507'::uuid,'01d89334-45a7-5217-86f7-e28f30184ca1'::uuid,'v0.1 assessment-informed draft',E'# ANNEX 2-W \\| CHAPTER 2 SOP AND FORM CROSS-REFERENCE REGISTER\n\n| SOP   | Primary forms / records                          | Related section          | Process owner                |\n|-------|--------------------------------------------------|--------------------------|------------------------------|\n| HR-01 | HR-F01; workforce plan; certified plantilla      | 2.8; 2.18; 2.21.1–2.21.2 | CDRRMO Head / HRMO           |\n| HR-02 | HR-F02; PDF and competency register              | 2.7; 2.9; 2.21.2         | HRMO / CDRRMO Head           |\n| HR-03 | HR-F02; recruitment and onboarding records       | 2.9; 2.14; 2.21.3        | HRMO                         |\n| HR-04 | HR-F03; TNA; L&D plan; competency register       | 2.12; 2.21.4             | CDRRMO Head / HRMO           |\n| HR-05 | HR-F04; duty roster; exception log               | 2.10–2.11; 2.21.5        | CDRRMO Head / division leads |\n| HR-06 | HR-F05; EOC designation register                 | 2.10; 2.21.5             | CDRRMO Head / EOC Manager    |\n| HR-07 | HR-F06; restricted referral record; PPE register | 2.13; 2.21.6             | CDRRMO Head / HRMO / OSH     |\n| HR-08 | HR-F07; SPMS records; HR-F08                     | 2.14; 2.17; 2.21.7       | CDRRMO Head / HRMO           |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch2_Annex_2-W.md (Chapter 2, v0.1 assessment-informed draft)','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('a206dcd0-633c-55d7-9426-66c9bb53b57a'::uuid,'77080547-c4e3-52f7-8908-02ce0fc78258'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'**CHAPTER 3**\n\n**RISK INTELLIGENCE, DRRM AND CONTINGENCY PLANNING,  \nAND INSTITUTIONAL PREPAREDNESS**\n\n**ORMOC CITY DISASTER RISK REDUCTION AND MANAGEMENT OFFICE (CDRRMO)  \nAND EMERGENCY OPERATIONS CENTER (EOC)**\n\n***Working Draft \\| 31 October 2026  \nSubject to technical, institutional, planning, legal and data validation***\n\n# DOCUMENT CONTROL\n\n| **Control field** | **Designation / requirement**                                                                           | **Control field**      | **Designation / requirement**                                |\n|-------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Document owner    | CDRRMO Head                                                                                             | Document custodian     | CDRRMO Research and Planning function                        |\n| Technical review  | CPDO; City Engineering; CENRO; City Agriculture; City Health; CSWDO; GIS/data custodians; relevant NGAs | Legal review           | City Legal Office                                            |\n| Planning review   | CPDO / Local Development Council / relevant sector offices                                              | Data governance review | City Data Protection Officer / data custodians               |\n| Approval          | Mayor / CDRRMC / Sanggunian, as legally applicable                                                      | Effective date         | Upon valid approval and issuance                             |\n| Review cycle      | Annual; after major hazard/data/planning changes; after exercises or disasters                          | Status                 | Controlled working draft; not yet an approved City directive |\n\n## REVISION AND APPROVAL RECORD\n\n| **Version** | **Date**        | **Description**                                                                                                                 | **Prepared / reviewed by**                           |\n|-------------|-----------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| 0.9         | 31 October 2026 | Rebuilt from project Chapter 1, Chapter 2, IAT v1.3, and verified official methodology sources; replaces generic workflow draft | For CDRRMO, CPDO, sector-office and legal validation |\n\n# CHAPTER 3 STRUCTURE\n\n- 3.1 Introduction and assessment-informed rationale\n\n- 3.2 Purpose and intended results\n\n- 3.3 Scope, boundaries and interfaces\n\n- 3.4 Legal, policy and methodological framework\n\n- 3.5 Systems-thinking and TQM operating principles\n\n- 3.6 Institutional roles and process ownership\n\n- 3.7 Risk information governance and authoritative hazard data\n\n- 3.8 Hazard information acquisition, validation and mapping\n\n- 3.9 Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment (CDRA)\n\n- 3.10 Exposure, vulnerability, loss and impact-chain analysis\n\n- 3.11 Critical facilities, lifelines and cascading-failure analysis\n\n- 3.12 Risk-informed planning and investment mainstreaming\n\n- 3.13 Contingency planning and exercise programme\n\n- 3.14 Public Service Continuity Planning (PSCP)\n\n- 3.15 Barangay DRRM planning and quality assurance\n\n- 3.16 Inclusive DRRM and ecosystem-based risk reduction\n\n- 3.17 Process workflows and decision controls\n\n- 3.18 Responsibility assignment (RACI)\n\n- 3.19 Performance monitoring, records and corrective action\n\n- 3.20 Priority implementation and validation agenda\n\n- 3.21 Conclusion\n\n- Annexes 3-A to 3-L: SOPs, forms, registers and traceability\n\n# 3.1 Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale\n\nThis chapter establishes the City''s operating system for understanding disaster and climate risk, translating risk evidence into land-use and development decisions, preparing for priority hazard scenarios, maintaining essential public services during disruption, and strengthening preparedness from city to barangay level. It treats risk intelligence as a continuous institutional process—not a one-time mapping or report-writing exercise.\n\nThe chapter is based on the October 2026 Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool (IAT) workbook, the governance and chapter architecture established in Chapter 1, and the process ownership and documented-control approach established in Chapter 2. The IAT is a diagnostic instrument. Its scores are not legal certification and do not independently establish that a plan, map, database or process is current, approved, complete or operational.\n\n![Figure 1: 3.1 Introduction and Assessment-informed Rationale](/library/ch3/fig-01.webp)\n\nThe assessment indicates that Ormoc has an existing CDRA, hazard information, a Local DRRM Plan and contingency-planning activity. The critical issue is the conversion of these assets into a coherent, maintained and decision-used system: complete multi-hazard coverage; technically validated exposure and vulnerability data; spatial integration into CLUP and zoning; risk screening of development proposals; tested hazard-specific contingency arrangements; and continuity arrangements for essential city services.\n\n# 3.2 Purpose and Intended Results\n\nThe purpose of this chapter is to standardize how Ormoc City identifies, assesses, communicates, plans for and periodically reviews disaster and climate risks. It shall produce the following institutional results:\n\n- An authoritative, version-controlled city risk information base, with source provenance, scale, date, limitations and responsible custodian for each dataset.\n\n- A technically defensible CDRA that follows the applicable DHSUD methodology and is spatially explicit, multi-hazard, multi-sectoral and usable for land-use and development decisions.\n\n- An approved and monitored LDRRMP aligned with the NDRRMP and integrated with the CLUP, CDP, LCCAP, ELA, LDIP, AIP and annual budget processes through CPDO-led planning mechanisms.\n\n- Hazard-specific contingency plans with clear scenario assumptions, objectives, roles, triggers, resource arrangements, information flows and exercise evidence.\n\n- A Public Service Continuity Plan based on an essential-functions inventory, business impact analysis, dependencies, continuity strategies, recovery priorities and tested arrangements.\n\n- A quality-assured system for reviewing barangay DRRM plans, including a verified barangay master list and documented feedback and follow-up.\n\n- Evidence that gender, age, disability, child protection, Indigenous Peoples'' rights and other contextually relevant inclusion requirements shape risk assessment, planning and service arrangements.\n\n# 3.3 Scope, Boundaries and Interfaces\n\nThis chapter applies to the CDRRMO Research and Planning function and all City offices that own, generate, validate, regulate, fund or use risk information and preparedness arrangements. It covers normal operations, heightened preparedness and anticipatory action, and readiness for response and continuity. It does not transfer the statutory or technical mandates of other offices to the CDRRMO.\n\n| **Institution / office**                                                           | **Accountability retained**                                                                                                                 | **Required interface with Chapter 3**                                                                     |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| CDRRMC                                                                             | Policy direction, approval/oversight functions assigned by law, monitoring and evaluation of LDRRMP                                         | Review risk evidence, endorse/recommend plans and monitor implementation                                  |\n| CDRRMO                                                                             | Facilitate risk assessment and contingency planning; consolidate local risk information; maintain local risk map; coordinate LDRRMP process | Own the risk-information and DRRM planning coordination process; maintain registers and technical records |\n| CPDO / LDC                                                                         | Local development planning, CLUP/CDP preparation and integration, investment prioritization                                                 | Lead formal mainstreaming of CDRA into CLUP/CDP and investment plans; convene planning review             |\n| Engineering / OBO / Zoning                                                         | Engineering, building-permit, locational-clearance, zoning and code decisions under their mandates                                          | Apply adopted hazard overlays, technical standards and permit conditions; retain regulatory decisions     |\n| CENRO / DENR / City Agriculture / sector offices                                   | Environmental, ecosystem, agriculture, health, social welfare, education and infrastructure mandates                                        | Provide authoritative sector data, impact analysis, service dependencies and technical validation         |\n| Budget / LFC / BAC / Accounting                                                    | Budget, financial management, procurement and accounting controls                                                                           | Apply approved risk-screening and budget controls; ensure proposals are fiscally and procedurally valid   |\n| HRMO / department heads                                                            | Personnel administration and office continuity responsibilities                                                                             | Identify critical functions, alternates, staffing, succession and employee welfare arrangements           |\n| Barangays / BDRRMCs                                                                | Barangay-level DRRM governance and plan implementation                                                                                      | Prepare and test BDRRMPs; validate local exposure, vulnerable households and evacuation information       |\n| OCD Region VIII / PAGASA / PHIVOLCS / MGB / GeoRiskPH and other competent agencies | National/regional mandates and authoritative technical products                                                                             | Provide source data, advisories, technical guidance and review as applicable                              |\n\n# 3.4 Legal, Policy and Methodological Framework\n\n| **Instrument / methodology**                                                   | **Operational relevance to this chapter**                                                                                                                                                                                                    | **Application / control**                                                                               |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| RA 10121 (2010), especially Sections 11–12                                     | LDRRMC oversight; LDRRMO functions include facilitating risk assessment and contingency planning, consolidating local risk information and maintaining a local risk map, and formulating an integrated LDRRMP in close coordination with LDC | Primary statutory basis; apply with its IRR and current official guidance                               |\n| RA 7160 (1991)                                                                 | Local planning, general welfare, local development and land-use planning powers                                                                                                                                                              | CPDO and competent regulatory offices retain their mandates                                             |\n| RA 9729, as amended by RA 10174                                                | Climate change adaptation and mainstreaming into local development                                                                                                                                                                           | Use climate projections and adaptation analysis within CDRA and plans                                   |\n| DHSUD MC No. 2025-004, Enhanced CDRA Policy                                    | Policy direction for standardized, updated, multi-hazard, multi-sectoral and spatially explicit CDRA                                                                                                                                         | Obtain and retain signed circular and annexes; follow current DHSUD process                             |\n| DHSUD CDRA training materials / 5-Step+ process                                | Hazard and climate information; impact scoping; exposure database; CDRA; MDA synthesis; compilation; CLUP mainstreaming; M&E; communication                                                                                                  | Methodological sequence for SOP 3-03; check technical manuals and prescribed tools                      |\n| RA 10173 and applicable NPC issuances                                          | Processing of personal and sensitive personal information                                                                                                                                                                                    | Apply purpose limitation, lawful basis, minimization, access control, retention and security            |\n| RA 9710; RA 7277 as amended; RA 9994; RA 10821; RA 8371; RA 11106              | Gender, disability, older persons, children, Indigenous Peoples and Filipino Sign Language inclusion                                                                                                                                         | Apply to data disaggregation, consultations, warnings, evacuation and continuity                        |\n| RA 12076 and its IRR                                                           | Permanent evacuation centre standards                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Coordinate facility planning and compliance; do not treat all buildings as suitable evacuation centres  |\n| DILG MC No. 2025-035 (Listo si KAP), Operation L!sto and current DILG guidance | Barangay preparedness and plan implementation                                                                                                                                                                                                | Use current issuance and quality-assurance tools; verify local implementation instructions              |\n| OCD/NDRRMC PSCP Guidebook (project reference; edition to be confirmed)         | Essential functions, business impact analysis, continuity strategies and recovery arrangements                                                                                                                                               | Use the actual guidebook copy as controlling technical reference; confirm edition and forms             |\n| NDRRMC/OCD contingency-planning guidance (exact edition to be confirmed)       | Scenario-based preparedness, response arrangements and plan testing                                                                                                                                                                          | CDRRMO shall obtain the controlling current manual and map its required steps before final SOP approval |\n| Sendai Framework 2015–2030; UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities     | Risk governance, understanding risk, resilience investment and city-level good practice                                                                                                                                                      | Benchmark only; does not replace Philippine law or national methodology                                 |\n\n**Source-control note:** The IAT References sheet lists some instruments as current but explicitly flags others for confirmation. Before approval, the CDRRMO Secretariat shall maintain certified copies or official links and record exact title, issuance number, date, version, issuing authority, amendments, custodian and review date. Where a source is named in the workbook but the actual operative text is not in the project file set (notably the current PSCP and contingency-planning manuals), this chapter identifies the dependency instead of attributing unverified step-by-step requirements to that source.\n\n# 3.5 Systems-Thinking and TQM Operating Principles\n\n| **Principle**               | **Required application**                                                                                                           | **Quality control**                                                                          |\n|-----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Risk as a system            | Assess hazard, exposure, vulnerability, capacity, land-use decisions, ecosystem functions and infrastructure dependencies together | Use impact chains and dependency maps; avoid isolated hazard maps as the sole decision basis |\n| Source provenance           | Every dataset has source agency, date, scale, coordinate reference system, method, limitations and custodian                       | Metadata completeness check; version register; traceable update history                      |\n| Decision-use orientation    | Risk analysis must inform a named decision, plan, investment, permit, preparedness action or continuity priority                   | Record the decision, decision owner, evidence considered and resulting action                |\n| Participation and inclusion | Engage affected communities and sector representatives in defining impacts, validating exposure and testing plans                  | Consultation records, disaggregated feedback and documented response to issues               |\n| PDCA / TQM                  | Plan data and analysis; do the assessment and planning; check quality and test arrangements; act on findings                       | Corrective-action register, audit trail, annual review and post-exercise update              |\n| Precaution and uncertainty  | Distinguish verified data, modelled estimates, assumptions and unknowns                                                            | State uncertainty and avoid presenting model outputs as parcel-level guarantees              |\n| No mandate transfer         | CDRRMO coordinates; competent offices validate and decide within their mandates                                                    | RACI reviewed by CLO and office heads; one accountable owner per decision                    |\n\n# 3.6 Institutional Roles and Process Ownership\n\n| **Process**                                       | **Accountable owner (proposed)**                                                       | **Responsible contributors / validators**                                   | **Approval or decision route**                                                                               |\n|---------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| City risk-information register and local risk map | CDRRMO Head                                                                            | Research and Planning; GIS/data custodians; source agencies; sector offices | CDRRMO administrative approval; technical sign-off by source/sector owners                                   |\n| CDRA preparation and update                       | CPDO for formal planning integration; CDRRMO for DRRM coordination                     | CDRA TWG; GIS; sector offices; barangays; competent NGAs                    | Technical validation by TWG; adoption/integration through applicable City planning and legislative processes |\n| CLUP/CDP mainstreaming                            | CPDO                                                                                   | CDRRMO, Engineering, Zoning, CENRO, sector offices, LDC                     | Formal CLUP/CDP and zoning approval processes                                                                |\n| LDRRMP and annual DRRM planning                   | CDRRMO Head / CDRRMC within their respective statutory roles                           | CPDO, departments, barangays, CSOs, private sector                          | CDRRMC approval/oversight as provided by RA 10121; local approval processes                                  |\n| Contingency plans                                 | CDRRMO Head as coordinating process owner; sector/incident lead per hazard             | Hazard technical agencies, IMT/response clusters, utilities, barangays      | CDRRMC/Mayor or designated authority according to applicable local issuance                                  |\n| PSCP                                              | City Administrator / Mayor-designated continuity executive (to be formally designated) | Department continuity coordinators; HRMO; ICT; Engineering; Budget; CDRRMO  | City executive approval; office heads accept function-specific responsibilities                              |\n| Barangay DRRM plan quality review                 | BDRRMC / Barangay Development Council within applicable law                            | CDRRMO and DILG review team; CSOs; vulnerable-sector representatives        | Barangay approval route under current law and DILG issuances                                                 |\n\nThe proposed accountable owners above are operational assignments for validation. They do not override legal authority, existing ordinances, approved organizational arrangements or the current DILG Listo si KAP framework. The City Legal Office shall confirm the precise barangay governance terminology and approval route before the final Manual is issued.\n\n# 3.7 Risk Information Governance and Authoritative Hazard Data\n\nThe CDRRMO shall maintain a City Risk Information Register and a controlled geospatial repository. It shall not independently re-author or downgrade authoritative hazard products. Source agencies retain responsibility for their technical products; the City records provenance, communicates limitations and determines how approved information is used in local planning.\n\n| **Data class**                                                                     | **Preferred source / custodian**                                            | **Required metadata and validation**                                                                                                     |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Tropical cyclone, rainfall, flood forecast and climate information                 | PAGASA; competent climate data providers; City engineering/drainage records | Product title, issue date, forecast/projection period, scenario, spatial resolution, units, baseline, uncertainty                        |\n| Ground shaking, fault, liquefaction, tsunami, volcanic hazards                     | DOST-PHIVOLCS / GeoRiskPH                                                   | Map/report title, scale, date, model assumptions, legend, hazard intensity, limitations and whether site-specific assessment is required |\n| Rain-induced landslide and geohazards                                              | DENR-MGB                                                                    | Map scale, susceptibility class, date, methodology, field validation status and limitations                                              |\n| Exposure: population, households, buildings, roads, facilities and economic assets | PSA/CBMS; CPDO; Assessor; Engineering; sector offices; barangays            | Reference date, geography, definitions, completeness, source, disaggregation, update frequency                                           |\n| Climate projections and ecosystem services                                         | DHSUD/CCC methodology; PAGASA; CENRO/DENR; City Agriculture                 | Emissions/scenario assumptions, time horizon, baseline, downscaling and uncertainty                                                      |\n| Critical infrastructure and lifelines                                              | Asset-owning departments, utilities, NGAs and facility operators            | Owner/operator, coordinates, capacity, condition, service area, dependencies, redundancy, restoration time and contact                   |\n\nNo single hazard portal or map layer should be treated as a complete site-specific engineering determination. Where a decision requires parcel-level, geotechnical, structural or other specialist assessment, the City shall obtain the assessment from the competent agency or licensed professional under the applicable regulatory process.\n\n# 3.8 Hazard Information Acquisition, Validation and Mapping\n\nThe mapping process is controlled by source provenance and fitness-for-purpose. The CDRRMO GIS/risk-information custodian shall maintain the layer catalogue; the originating agency remains the technical source. A map shall not be published as an official City planning layer until its source, date, scale, coordinate system, legend, geographic coverage and limitations have been checked and the relevant technical office has signed the validation record.\n\n1.  Register the request and define the decision purpose, hazard, geographic extent, map scale and intended users. Reject requests that do not specify whether the output is for screening, planning, public information or site-specific technical review.\n\n2.  Acquire the current official dataset from the responsible agency or its designated portal. Retain the original download, accompanying metadata, readme, legends, technical notes and correspondence; record access date and version.\n\n3.  Check completeness and integrity: file opens; geometry is valid; attributes and classes are present; units and coordinate reference system are known; coverage includes the required City/barangay extent; and metadata identifies scale and date.\n\n4.  Compare the product with other authoritative layers and local evidence without averaging or arbitrarily choosing conflicting values. Log discrepancies, identify which agency owns the disputed technical interpretation, and request clarification or a revised product.\n\n5.  Transform or reproject a working copy only when necessary, document the original and target coordinate reference systems, transformation method and software/version, and preserve the unmodified source file.\n\n6.  Overlay the hazard layer with the authoritative barangay boundary, exposure units and critical assets. Record the spatial operation, date, data versions, treatment of no-data areas and any assumptions.\n\n7.  Submit the draft map to the relevant technical office and source agency for validation where needed. The review must check scale suitability, legend, interpretation, local anomalies, uncertainty and whether the layer is suitable for the stated decision.\n\n8.  Approve the map for a defined use category: internal screening, public information, CLUP/CDP planning layer, or regulatory use. Regulatory use requires the appropriate CPDO/Zoning/Engineering/legal and formal adoption route.\n\n9.  Publish the approved version with map title, date, source, scale, legend, limitations, contact and version number. Archive superseded versions and notify users when a material revision occurs.\n\n**Decision control**: If source data conflict, are outdated, have inadequate scale, or leave an unmapped area, the map custodian shall mark the limitation and refer the issue to the source agency and relevant technical office. The CDRRMO shall not fill a gap by drawing an unverified hazard boundary. The output is a controlled map package: source files, metadata, validation record, map layout, approval/use classification, publication record and revision entry.\n\n# 3.9 Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment (CDRA)\n\nOrmoc shall conduct and update its CDRA using the applicable DHSUD Enhanced CDRA Policy and current official CDRA training and technical materials. The official DHSUD learning material describes a 5-Step+ approach: (1) collect and organize climate-change and hazard information; (2) scope potential impacts; (3) develop an exposure database; (4) conduct the CDRA; and (5) summarize findings, followed by compilation, CLUP mainstreaming, monitoring and evaluation, and communication planning. The City shall use the current prescribed tools, definitions and technical parameters rather than replacing them with an internally invented scoring model.\n\n| **Method stage**                                        | **Required substantive work**                                                                                                                                                                                                 | **Required output / quality gate**                                                                               |\n|---------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1\\. Collect and organize hazard and climate information | Identify priority hazards and climate stressors; acquire authoritative current and future hazard/climate information; document spatial scale, temporal horizon, scenario, baseline and limitations                            | Hazard/climate information inventory; metadata; agreed assessment scope; gap register                            |\n| 2\\. Scope potential impacts                             | For each hazard/climate stressor, identify affected sectors, elements at risk, impact pathways, spatial extent, relevant time horizon and local context; consult technical offices and communities                            | Hazard-impact scoping matrix and documented impact chains                                                        |\n| 3\\. Develop exposure database                           | Build georeferenced exposure units for population, households, buildings, infrastructure, critical facilities, livelihoods, agriculture, ecosystems and relevant cultural assets; standardize identifiers and definitions     | Validated exposure geodatabase and attribute dictionary; completeness and privacy checks                         |\n| 4\\. Conduct CDRA                                        | Apply the official method for vulnerability/sensitivity, adaptive capacity and disaster risk assessment; use the prescribed hazard-specific parameters and available climate projections; retain calculations and assumptions | Hazard/sector risk assessment tables, maps, calculation files, uncertainty statement and validation record       |\n| 5\\. Summarize findings and MDAs                         | Synthesize risk levels and identify Major Decision Areas and corresponding policy interventions; connect findings to land-use, development, infrastructure, social protection and ecosystem decisions                         | MDA register with spatial reference, evidence, affected elements, policy intervention, lead office and indicator |\n| 6\\. Compile results                                     | Prepare the CDRA report, map atlas, geodatabase, technical annexes, consultation records, data dictionary and executive summary                                                                                               | Complete, quality-assured CDRA package                                                                           |\n| 7\\. Mainstream in CLUP and development planning         | Translate risk findings and MDAs into land-use policies, zoning overlays, development controls, programs, projects and investment priorities through CPDO-led processes                                                       | CDRA-to-CLUP/CDP crosswalk; draft policy and zoning amendments; investment tags                                  |\n| 8\\. Monitor and evaluate                                | Define indicators, data owners, update frequency and triggers for reassessment; monitor hazard, exposure, vulnerability and implementation changes                                                                            | CDRA M&E matrix and annual update record                                                                         |\n| 9\\. Communicate                                         | Prepare audience-specific products for decision-makers, barangays, communities, sector groups and the public; explain uncertainty and limitations                                                                             | Communication plan, accessible maps/briefs and consultation feedback                                             |\n\nOrmoc-specific completion requirements. The existing CDRA is reported in the IAT as covering flood, rain-induced landslides and storm surge. The workbook identifies earthquake, liquefaction and tsunami assessment as outstanding. The City shall confirm whether additional hazards (including tropical cyclone wind/rain impacts, drought/heat, technological/industrial and geothermal-related scenarios) are required by the updated risk profile and official methodology. It shall also correct or verify climate-projection tables carrying Naval/Biliran labels before any such table is reused. No numerical exposure or loss figure from the earlier report should be republished until reconciled against the source table, geographic coverage and current master datasets.\n\n# 3.10 Exposure, Vulnerability, Loss and Impact-chain Analysis\n\nThe exposure database shall identify what is located in hazard-affected areas; vulnerability analysis shall explain why and how exposed elements may be harmed; capacity analysis shall identify resources and adaptive capabilities that reduce impacts. The analysis must not treat exposure as synonymous with risk. It must include direct and, where data permit, indirect and cascading impacts.\n\n| **Dimension**                       | **Minimum analytical content for Ormoc**                                                                                                                                                                 | **Primary data owners / contributors**                                |\n|-------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Population and households           | Population by barangay and relevant sex, age, disability, pregnancy/lactation, child, older-person and Indigenous Peoples'' variables where lawfully collected; household location and access constraints | CPDO/CBMS custodian, PSA, CSWDO, City Health, barangays               |\n| Housing and buildings               | Building use, occupancy, construction/material, floor level where relevant, condition, tenure/informal settlement and critical building function                                                         | Assessor, Engineering, OBO, CPDO, barangays                           |\n| Livelihoods and economic activity   | Employment, income sources, market access, business interruption, agriculture/fisheries, tourism and informal livelihoods                                                                                | City Agriculture, market/economic offices, DTI, CPDO, business groups |\n| Infrastructure and services         | Roads, bridges, ports, power, water, wastewater, telecommunications, fuel, hospitals, schools, evacuation centres and EOC                                                                                | Asset-owning departments, utilities, NGAs                             |\n| Ecosystems and natural capital      | Watersheds, forests, mangroves, coastal ecosystems, wetlands, critical habitats and ecosystem services that reduce hazard impacts                                                                        | CENRO/DENR, City Agriculture, CPDO                                    |\n| Cultural heritage and social assets | Heritage overlay assets, culturally significant sites, community facilities and displacement impacts                                                                                                     | CPDO, culture/tourism offices, barangays, IP representatives          |\n| Vulnerability and capacity          | Physical fragility, access, poverty, social exclusion, service access, coping capacity, preparedness and adaptive capacity                                                                               | Sector offices, CSOs, barangays, community validation                 |\n\nPersonal and sensitive personal information shall not be placed in public-facing GIS layers or general-purpose map exports. Use aggregated or appropriately de-identified information for planning and publication. Household-level targeting data must be held by authorized custodians under a documented data-sharing and access-control arrangement consistent with RA 10173.\n\n# 3.11 Critical Facilities, Lifelines and Cascading-Failure Analysis\n\nThe existing CDRA facility and lifeline inventory is the starting point, not the completed dependency analysis. The IAT identifies that facilities and lifelines have been listed but interdependencies and failure chains have not yet been mapped or used to set protection and restoration priorities.\n\n10. Confirm the asset universe and unique asset identifier with each owner/operator. At minimum include EOC, hospitals, health centres, evacuation centres, schools used for authorized emergency functions, bridges, major roads, port, water supply, wastewater, power, telecommunications, fuel and key government facilities.\n\n11. For each asset, record location, owner/operator, service function, capacity, service population/area, operating hours, condition, hazard exposure, vulnerability, backup arrangements, access routes, critical inputs and restoration dependencies.\n\n12. Identify upstream and downstream dependencies. Examples to test—not presume—include power loss affecting water pumping, telecommunications and hospital operations; road/bridge disruption isolating evacuation centres; fuel disruption affecting generators, ambulances and water delivery; and EOC communications failure affecting coordination.\n\n13. Construct directed dependency maps and scenario-based failure chains. For each chain, identify initiating hazard, asset failure, service consequence, affected population, duration, cascading assets and alternative arrangements.\n\n14. Validate each dependency with the asset owner/operator and affected service providers. Record disputed or unknown links as data gaps; do not convert assumptions into confirmed dependencies.\n\n15. Prioritize risk treatment and continuity options using consequence, likelihood, service criticality, redundancy, restoration time and vulnerable-population impact. Use approved criteria and document any local weighting adopted.\n\n16. Integrate priority actions into asset management, capital investment, contingency plans and PSCP. Maintain a restoration-priority list with accountable owner, target, funding route and evidence of completion.\n\nThe output shall be a Critical Facilities and Lifelines Register, dependency diagram, failure-chain scenario sheets, and a protection/restoration priority register. Asset owners remain accountable for the accuracy and operation of their assets.\n\n# 3.12 Risk-Informed Planning and Investment Mainstreaming\n\nThe IAT identifies the CLUP 2017–2027 as predating the CDRA and notes that the CDRA results have not been demonstrably translated into current zoning overlays or a formal project/budget screening step. This chapter therefore establishes an interface, not a transfer of CPDO or Budget Office mandates.\n\n17. CPDO convenes a planning integration review using the approved CDRA package, MDA register, hazard maps, exposure database, critical-facility dependencies and barangay validation.\n\n18. Each MDA is mapped to the relevant CLUP land-use policy, zoning provision, CDP outcome, LCCAP action, ELA priority, LDIP/AIP proposal and responsible implementing office.\n\n19. Where a hazard or climate risk affects land-use suitability, CPDO and the Zoning Administrator lead the assessment of land-use policy and zoning options; Engineering/OBO and other regulators determine applicable technical standards and permit conditions.\n\n20. Before an investment proposal is prioritized, the originating office completes the DRR/CCA screening record. It identifies hazard exposure, climate sensitivity, affected people/assets, design and siting implications, ecosystem effects, residual risk and proposed risk treatment.\n\n21. CPDO reviews consistency with plans and spatial policy; Engineering/OBO/Zoning review technical and regulatory matters; CDRRMO provides risk evidence and DRRM advice; Budget/LFC assess fiscal and budget requirements; the competent approving body decides.\n\n22. Record screening outcomes in the project appraisal and LDC/LFC minutes. Proposals with unresolved material risk shall be returned for redesign, specialist assessment or documented decision by the competent authority; the CDRRMO does not independently veto statutory approvals.\n\n23. Monitor whether adopted measures are implemented and whether risk exposure is reduced. Feed permit, project and loss information back into the next CDRA update.\n\n| **Screening question**                                            | **Minimum evidence to attach**                                                                       | **Review lead**                         |\n|-------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|\n| Is the site or service exposed to current or future hazards?      | Official hazard overlay, map date/scale, climate scenario/time horizon                               | CPDO with CDRRMO technical input        |\n| Could the project increase exposure or vulnerability elsewhere?   | Site alternatives, drainage/downstream effects, access and displacement analysis                     | CPDO / Engineering / CENRO              |\n| Are design, siting and operational standards adequate?            | Applicable code, engineering/geotechnical assessment, service continuity and residual-risk statement | Engineering / OBO / competent regulator |\n| Are vulnerable groups disproportionately affected?                | Disaggregated impact analysis, accessible consultation and mitigation measures                       | CSWDO / GAD / sector offices            |\n| Does the project affect protective ecosystems or cultural assets? | Ecosystem service and heritage screening; permits/clearances as applicable                           | CENRO / DENR / CPDO                     |\n| Is the risk treatment funded and assigned?                        | Costed mitigation, responsible office, implementation schedule and monitoring indicator              | Originating office / Budget / LFC       |\n\n# 3.13 Contingency planning and exercise programme\n\nContingency planning is a management process for analyzing specific potential events or emerging situations and establishing arrangements in advance for timely, effective and appropriate response. RA 10121 requires the LDRRMO to facilitate and support local contingency planning; the LDRRMC must regularly review and test the LDRRMP. The detailed hazard-specific methodology must be checked against the current OCD/NDRRMC contingency-planning guide held by the City. This chapter sets the minimum City control architecture and does not claim that the sequence below reproduces an unverified manual verbatim.\n\n| **Plan component**               | **Required content**                                                                                                                                   |\n|----------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Purpose and scope                | Hazard, geographic coverage, planning assumptions, plan owner, version, validity and relationship to LDRRMP/EOC/ICS                                    |\n| Risk scenario                    | Most-probable and credible severe scenario, source evidence, time/place, onset, magnitude, cascading effects and uncertainty                           |\n| Planning assumptions             | Forecast/monitoring basis, warning lead time, access, population, service and resource assumptions; assumptions requiring confirmation                 |\n| Objectives and priorities        | Life safety, evacuation, protection of critical assets, continuity of essential services, protection of vulnerable groups and environmental safeguards |\n| Concept of operations            | Preparedness, activation, response, stabilization and transition; coordination with incident command and EOC; no displacement of field command         |\n| Roles and coordination           | Lead/support agencies, incident command/IMT interface, cluster/sector responsibilities, barangay roles and escalation routes                           |\n| Triggers and decision points     | Authoritative warning/advisory thresholds and locally approved action triggers; source and authority for each trigger; avoid invented thresholds       |\n| Operational arrangements         | Warning, evacuation, shelter/CCCM, search and rescue, health, protection, logistics, public information, RDANA and service restoration                 |\n| Resources and gaps               | Personnel, equipment, supplies, facilities, transport, communications, suppliers, prepositioning, mutual aid and request process                       |\n| Information and reporting        | Situation reports, common operating picture, decision log, data validation, public information approval and reporting frequency                        |\n| Safety, inclusion and protection | Accessible warning, safe evacuation, child protection, gender and protection safeguards, PWD access, older persons, IP and MHPSS                       |\n| Exercise, review and update      | Tabletop/functional/full-scale exercise objectives, evaluation, corrective action owner, deadline and plan revision                                    |\n\nPriority hazard portfolio for validation. The IAT names flood/flash flood, landslide, earthquake/tsunami, tropical cyclone and industrial/geothermal hazards. The City shall confirm priority and scenario scope using the current CDRA, official agency products, historical impact records and sector risk. A single generic all-hazards plan is not a substitute for hazard-specific operational annexes where triggers, warning lead times, response tactics or technical agencies differ.\n\nExercise control: Each approved plan shall have an exercise schedule and documented test. At minimum, the CDRRMO shall ensure that the plan is walked through with assigned agencies and barangays, that contact and resource arrangements are checked, and that decisions and gaps are recorded. Exercise type and frequency shall be set in the approved annual programme and applicable national guidance. A plan is not “tested” merely because a meeting or orientation occurred.\n\n# 3.14 Public Service Continuity Planning (PSCP)\n\nThe IAT records the PSCP as not completed and no business impact analysis or essential-functions list sighted. The City shall therefore treat PSCP as a priority institutional deliverable. The PSCP shall address continuity of essential City functions during disruption, including the CDRRMO/EOC and services whose interruption may create cascading impacts on life safety and public welfare.\n\n| **PSCP element**               | **Required procedure and output**                                                                                                                                                                 |\n|--------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Governance and scope           | Mayor/City Administrator designates continuity executive, department continuity coordinators, plan custodian and approval route; define covered offices and essential services                    |\n| Essential-function inventory   | Each department identifies legal mandate, service users, minimum service level, process owner, key staff, systems, facilities, records, suppliers and interdependencies                           |\n| Business impact analysis (BIA) | Assess disruption consequences over time; identify maximum tolerable disruption and recovery priorities using an approved City method; distinguish statutory deadlines from service-level choices |\n| Dependency analysis            | Map staff, ICT, power, water, telecom, transport, facilities, records, suppliers, fuel and inter-office dependencies; validate with owners                                                        |\n| Continuity strategies          | Identify alternate work locations, remote/manual procedures, backup power/communications, data backup, alternate suppliers, mutual aid and staff succession                                       |\n| Plan development               | Define activation authority, notification, relocation, service prioritization, internal/external communication, resource control and deactivation                                                 |\n| Testing and maintenance        | Exercise continuity scenarios, document performance, correct gaps, review after organizational/technology changes and update contact lists                                                        |\n\nThe City shall not invent universal recovery time objectives without a BIA. Each department shall propose service-specific priorities and tolerable disruption periods based on statutory obligations, life-safety consequences, affected population and operational dependencies; the designated continuity executive shall review and approve the city-wide prioritization framework. The PSCP shall link with Chapter 6 EOC alternate-site and communications arrangements but shall not make the EOC the sole continuity solution for all City services.\n\n# 3.15 Barangay DRRM planning and quality assurance\n\nRA 10121 establishes a BDRRMC in every barangay and assigns local DRRM planning and coordination functions. The IAT refers to 85 barangays, while the CLUP and other project records refer to 110. The Manual shall not settle this discrepancy by assumption. CPDO, the City Secretary and DILG City Local Government Operations Office shall reconcile the authoritative current barangay master list and document the basis before the coverage denominator is used in official reporting.\n\n24. Maintain one master register of barangays with official name, geographic identifier, legal status, boundary reference, population reference year and responsible BDRRMC contact.\n\n25. Request the current BDRRMP and approval evidence from each barangay using the current DILG/OCD-prescribed template and review tool. Record the document version, coverage period, approval date and evidence source.\n\n26. Conduct a completeness and quality review covering risk profile, priority hazards, exposed and vulnerable groups, early warning, evacuation, response arrangements, resources, annual work programme, budget, inclusion and monitoring.\n\n27. Return each plan with specific, actionable comments linked to the applicable quality criteria; identify mandatory corrections separately from recommended improvements.\n\n28. Verify that comments have been addressed and record the disposition. A submitted plan is not counted as quality-assured merely because it has been received.\n\n29. Consolidate status by barangay: not submitted, submitted, under review, returned for revision, technically accepted, approved, tested, and due for update.\n\n30. Provide technical assistance and schedule plan-testing support. Link barangay plans with City hazard scenarios, evacuation arrangements, warning protocols and resource requests without removing barangay ownership.\n\n31. Report gaps and overdue actions to the CDRRMC and DILG through the applicable channel; maintain a follow-up register.\n\n# 3.16 Inclusive DRRM and ecosystem-based risk reduction\n\nInclusion is a design requirement across the risk-information and preparedness cycle, not a stand-alone consultation event. Data collection must be lawful and proportionate; consultation must be accessible; and the analysis must show how different groups experience exposure, sensitivity, barriers and capacities.\n\n| **Area**                               | **Minimum operating requirement**                                                                                                         | **Evidence**                                                                        |\n|----------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Gender and women                       | Analyze differentiated impacts, care responsibilities, safety, livelihoods and participation; integrate GAD planning and budgeting        | Disaggregated analysis, consultation records, GAD-tagged actions                    |\n| Children                               | Apply child-sensitive risk analysis, protection, education continuity and safe evacuation arrangements                                    | Child-focused impact analysis, CSWDO/DepEd validation, child protection measures    |\n| Persons with disabilities              | Identify functional/access requirements, accessible warning, transport, shelter and communication needs with consent and privacy controls | PWD organization consultation, accessible plan/map formats, facility access review  |\n| Older persons                          | Assess mobility, health, medication, support networks and accessible service arrangements                                                 | Sector validation, evacuation and continuity arrangements                           |\n| Indigenous Peoples                     | Respect IPRA rights, cultural protocols, Indigenous Knowledge Systems and Practices, and meaningful participation                         | Consultation record, appropriate consent/process, culturally relevant risk analysis |\n| Pregnant/lactating persons and infants | Include maternal, newborn and infant feeding needs in evacuation, health and continuity planning                                          | Health-office validation and service protocols                                      |\n| Ecosystems                             | Treat watershed, upland forest, mangroves, wetlands and coastal ecosystems as risk-reduction assets and sources of ecosystem services     | Ecosystem inventory, condition monitoring, restoration/protection actions           |\n\nThe IAT identifies the upper Anilao–Malbasag watershed, upland forest cover and coastal mangroves as important protective ecosystems. The assessment notes that these are identified in plans but are not yet monitored consistently as risk-reduction assets. CENRO/DENR and relevant offices shall define measurable condition indicators and a monitoring programme; the CDRRMO shall incorporate relevant findings into risk analysis and planning, not assume responsibility for environmental regulation.\n\n# 3.17 Process workflows and decision controls\n\nThe workflows below are operational control procedures derived from the legal mandate, the official CDRA sequence and the City''s documented assessment needs. They identify actors, actions, decision gates and records. Where an external manual contains a more specific mandatory step, the controlling manual shall prevail and the local SOP shall be revised to reflect it.\n\n## SOP 3-01 \\| Hazard data acquisition, validation and map release\n\nProcess owner: CDRRMO Research and Planning / GIS custodian. Purpose: Obtain authoritative, current and fit-for-purpose hazard information and control its use.\n\n| **Step / control point**     | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                                                              | **Mandatory record / output**                      |\n|------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|\n| Open and scope request       | CDRRMO records requester, purpose, hazard, geography, scale, deadline and intended decision. Return incomplete requests for clarification.                                                         | Risk-information request and tracking number       |\n| Acquire source product       | GIS custodian obtains current product from source agency/official portal; preserves original file, metadata, legend, technical note and access date.                                               | Source dataset and acquisition log                 |\n| Check metadata and integrity | Check date, scale, CRS, units, geometry, attributes, legend, geographic coverage, no-data areas, model assumptions and limitations.                                                                | Completed metadata/quality checklist               |\n| Resolve discrepancies        | Compare authoritative sources and local evidence; record differences; refer technical interpretation to source agency and competent sector office; do not average or redraw uncertain boundaries.  | Discrepancy log and source-agency response         |\n| Prepare controlled overlay   | Use approved barangay boundaries and exposure layers; document reprojection, spatial operation, data versions and processing software.                                                             | Working GIS layer and processing record            |\n| Technical validation         | Relevant technical office checks interpretation and fitness for purpose; source agency consulted for material uncertainty; CPDO/Zoning/Engineering review if intended for planning/regulatory use. | Signed technical validation                        |\n| Classify and approve use     | Assign use category (internal screening, public information, planning layer, regulatory layer); route regulatory use through formal adoption process.                                              | Approval/use classification record                 |\n| Publish and maintain         | Publish map with date, source, scale, legend, limitations and version; archive superseded version and notify users of material changes.                                                            | Released map, publication log and version register |\n\n## SOP 3-02 \\| Risk information and geospatial repository maintenance\n\nProcess owner: CDRRMO Head / designated data custodian. Purpose: Maintain a controlled, auditable city risk information base.\n\n| **Step / control point**   | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                   | **Mandatory record / output**           |\n|----------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|\n| Register datasets          | Assign dataset ID, title, source owner, custodian, date, geography, scale, CRS, update cycle, sensitivity and permitted use.                            | Risk Information Register               |\n| Apply data governance      | Classify public, internal, confidential or personal/sensitive data; establish access rights, lawful purpose, retention and sharing conditions.          | Data classification and access register |\n| Validate updates           | Compare incoming version with existing data; identify changed attributes/geometries, missing records and implications for previously issued maps/plans. | Change log and validation note          |\n| Release controlled version | Assign version, approval status and effective date; preserve source file and processing history; distribute only approved products.                     | Versioned dataset and distribution log  |\n| Review and archive         | Review at the defined cycle and after major events, agency revisions or boundary/asset changes; archive superseded versions and record reason.          | Review record and archive index         |\n\n## SOP 3-03 \\| City Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment\n\nProcess owner: CPDO-led CDRA process; CDRRMO coordinates DRRM risk evidence. Purpose: Produce a method-compliant CDRA that supports CLUP and development decisions.\n\n| **Step / control point**                  | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                                | **Mandatory record / output**                              |\n|-------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Approve assessment protocol               | CPDO and CDRRMO define scope, planning horizon, hazards, sectors, geography, technical team, data needs, prescribed DHSUD tools and consultation plan.               | Approved CDRA inception/protocol                           |\n| Collect and organize information (Step 1) | Acquire authoritative hazard and climate data; document baseline, scenarios, time horizons, resolution, metadata and limitations; identify data gaps.                | Hazard/climate inventory and data-gap register             |\n| Scope potential impacts (Step 2)          | For each hazard/stressor, identify potential impacts by sector and element at risk; construct impact chains; validate with sector offices and communities.           | Hazard-impact scoping matrix                               |\n| Develop exposure database (Step 3)        | Build georeferenced exposure units; standardize IDs and attributes; validate spatial joins, completeness, reference dates and privacy controls.                      | Exposure geodatabase and data dictionary                   |\n| Conduct risk assessment (Step 4)          | Apply current DHSUD prescribed vulnerability and risk methods, parameters and tools; retain calculations, assumptions and uncertainty; have sector experts validate. | Risk tables, maps, calculation files and validation record |\n| Summarize findings and MDAs (Step 5)      | Synthesize risk results into Major Decision Areas and policy interventions; identify affected places, groups, systems and responsible planning decisions.            | MDA register and policy-intervention matrix                |\n| Compile results (Step 6)                  | Prepare report, map atlas, geodatabase, technical annexes, data sources, consultation records and executive summary; correct inconsistencies before sign-off.        | Complete CDRA package                                      |\n| Mainstream into CLUP (Step 7)             | CPDO translates MDAs into spatial policies, land-use suitability, overlays, zoning and development control proposals through formal planning process.                | CDRA-to-CLUP crosswalk and proposed amendments             |\n| Monitor and evaluate (Step 8)             | Assign indicators, data owners and review frequency; update after material hazard, exposure, vulnerability, climate or land-use change.                              | CDRA M&E matrix and update log                             |\n| Communicate (Step 9)                      | Prepare accessible, audience-specific risk communication and explain uncertainty, implications and action choices.                                                   | CDRA communication products and feedback record            |\n\n## SOP 3-04 \\| Critical facilities and dependency/failure-chain analysis\n\nProcess owner: CDRRMO coordinates; asset owners are accountable for asset data. Purpose: Identify critical service dependencies and inform protection and restoration priorities.\n\n| **Step / control point** | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                         | **Mandatory record / output**              |\n|--------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|\n| Define asset scope       | Confirm critical services and asset categories with department heads, utilities and NGAs; assign asset owner and unique ID.                                   | Approved asset scope and owner list        |\n| Inventory assets         | Record coordinates, function, capacity, condition, hazard exposure, service population, operator and alternate arrangements.                                  | Critical Facilities and Lifelines Register |\n| Map dependencies         | Identify required inputs, upstream services, downstream users, access routes, communications, fuel, staff, ICT and utilities.                                 | Dependency matrix and network diagram      |\n| Develop failure chains   | For priority scenarios, trace initiating hazard through asset failure to service disruption and population consequence; mark unverified links as assumptions. | Failure-chain scenario sheets              |\n| Validate with operators  | Conduct structured validation with asset owners and service providers; correct errors and document unknowns.                                                  | Signed validation records                  |\n| Prioritize treatments    | Apply approved criteria to consequence, likelihood, criticality, redundancy, recovery time and vulnerable-group effects; document rationale.                  | Protection/restoration priority register   |\n| Integrate and monitor    | Transfer actions to PSCP, contingency plans, asset plans and AIP/LDIP; monitor completion and service resilience.                                             | Action register and monitoring report      |\n\n## SOP 3-05 \\| LDRRMP and DRR/CCA mainstreaming interface\n\nProcess owner: CDRRMO coordinates LDRRMP; CPDO leads development-plan integration. Purpose: Ensure risk evidence is converted into plans, programmes and budget decisions.\n\n| **Step / control point**                | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                         | **Mandatory record / output**                           |\n|-----------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|\n| Compile planning evidence               | CDRRMO provides approved CDRA, maps, MDA register, contingency findings, losses, ecosystem and dependency analysis.                           | Planning evidence pack                                  |\n| Convene planning review                 | CPDO convenes LDC and sector offices; review existing CLUP/CDP/LCCAP/ELA/LDIP/AIP and identify policy/programme gaps.                         | Planning review minutes and gap matrix                  |\n| Set objectives and interventions        | Translate risk findings into prevention, mitigation, preparedness and adaptation outcomes; assign measurable outputs and responsible offices. | Results framework and intervention matrix               |\n| Screen spatial and investment proposals | Apply DRR/CCA screening to proposed sites, designs and service models; route technical/regulatory questions to competent offices.             | Completed screening form and technical comments         |\n| Prioritize and resource                 | CPDO/LDC and Budget/LFC use applicable planning and budget processes to prioritize viable, costed interventions.                              | Prioritized project list and budget records             |\n| Approve, implement and monitor          | Submit plans and proposals through statutory approval routes; track outputs, outcomes, risk reduction and corrective action.                  | Approved plans, implementation reports and M&E register |\n\n## SOP 3-06 \\| Hazard-specific contingency plan development and control\n\nProcess owner: CDRRMO as coordinating process owner; hazard/sector leads as technical owners. Purpose: Prepare and maintain tested plans for priority hazard scenarios.\n\n| **Step / control point**                       | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                          | **Mandatory record / output**                          |\n|------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|\n| Select priority scenario                       | Use current CDRA, authoritative hazard information, historical impacts and sector risk; record why the hazard/scenario is prioritized.                         | Scenario selection note                                |\n| Confirm planning team and source guidance      | Designate lead/support offices, barangay and community participants; obtain current OCD/NDRRMC contingency-planning manual and applicable sector protocols.    | Team designation and source register                   |\n| Build scenario and assumptions                 | Define plausible scenario, location, magnitude, timing, warning lead time, exposed population, cascading impacts and uncertainty using authoritative evidence. | Scenario and assumptions sheet                         |\n| Set objectives and concept of operations       | Define operational priorities, phases, coordination, field command/EOC interface, escalation and transition to recovery.                                       | Concept of operations                                  |\n| Assign tasks and resources                     | Specify lead/support agency, task, trigger, resource, communication, reporting and request route; validate contacts and capacity.                              | Task matrix, resource gap and contact registers        |\n| Complete protection and inclusion arrangements | Set warning accessibility, evacuation, shelter/CCCM, child protection, health, MHPSS, disability access and environmental safeguards.                          | Protection/inclusion checklist and operational annexes |\n| Validate and approve                           | Conduct technical workshop/tabletop; resolve contradictions; obtain approval through the City route established by current issuance.                           | Validation minutes and approved plan                   |\n| Exercise and correct                           | Conduct scheduled exercise; evaluate objectives and decision points; assign corrective action owner and deadline; update plan version.                         | Exercise report and corrective-action register         |\n| Review and maintain                            | Review after exercise, event, material change in hazard data, staffing, resource or contact arrangements and at the approved periodic interval.                | Annual review and revision history                     |\n\n## SOP 3-07 \\| Public Service Continuity Planning\n\nProcess owner: City continuity executive to be designated; department heads own service continuity. Purpose: Protect essential City functions during disruption.\n\n| **Step / control point**         | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                               | **Mandatory record / output**                 |\n|----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| Establish governance             | Mayor/City Administrator designates continuity executive, coordinators, plan custodian, activation authority and approval route.                                    | Designation/authority record                  |\n| Inventory essential functions    | Each department identifies services, legal basis, service users, minimum service level, process owner, staff, records, facilities, ICT and suppliers.               | Essential Functions Inventory                 |\n| Conduct BIA                      | Assess disruption impacts over time, statutory deadlines, life-safety and welfare consequences, dependencies and tolerable disruption; justify recovery priorities. | BIA worksheet and validated priority schedule |\n| Analyze dependencies and risks   | Map staff, power, water, telecom, ICT, facilities, transport, records, suppliers, fuel and inter-office dependencies; test single points of failure.                | Dependency and continuity risk register       |\n| Select continuity strategies     | Specify alternate site/work arrangements, manual workarounds, backup systems, staff succession, alternate suppliers, mutual aid and communication methods.          | Continuity strategy matrix                    |\n| Prepare departmental plans       | Set activation, notification, relocation, service prioritization, resource allocation, external communication, record protection and deactivation procedures.       | Department continuity plans                   |\n| Integrate city-wide arrangements | Resolve cross-department dependencies; coordinate with EOC alternate-site arrangements and emergency response plans; define city-wide priorities.                   | Consolidated PSCP                             |\n| Approve and exercise             | Approve through designated executive route; test realistic disruption scenarios and evaluate service restoration.                                                   | Approved PSCP and exercise report             |\n| Review and update                | Update after exercise, incident, staff/technology/site/supplier changes and periodic review; close corrective actions.                                              | Revision log and corrective-action register   |\n\n## SOP 3-08 \\| Barangay DRRM plan review and quality assurance\n\nProcess owner: Barangay plan owner: BDRRMC/Barangay Development Council; City reviewer: CDRRMO. Purpose: Maintain a verified and quality-assured city-wide BDRRMP status system.\n\n| **Step / control point**       | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                                               | **Mandatory record / output**           |\n|--------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|\n| Reconcile barangay master list | CPDO, City Secretary and DILG City LGOO validate official barangay count, names, identifiers and boundaries; record authoritative source.                           | Signed barangay master list             |\n| Call for current plans         | Issue formal request with current DILG/OCD template, quality tool, required approval evidence and submission deadline.                                              | Submission notice and receipt log       |\n| Screen completeness            | Check plan period, approval, risk profile, priority hazards, objectives, projects, budgets, roles, warning, evacuation, inclusion and monitoring.                   | Completeness checklist                  |\n| Conduct quality assessment     | Use the current official Quality Assessment System/Quality Assessment Tool and Listo si KAP guidance where applicable; do not substitute an unapproved local score. | Completed official review tool          |\n| Return and support revision    | Provide itemized comments, technical assistance and a revision due date; separate mandatory criteria from recommendations.                                          | Review letter and assistance record     |\n| Verify correction and status   | Recheck evidence and record status; do not count submission as approval or quality acceptance.                                                                      | Barangay plan status matrix             |\n| Consolidate and report         | Identify coverage gaps, recurring deficiencies, resource needs and plans requiring exercise; report to CDRRMC/DILG as applicable.                                   | Consolidated report and action register |\n| Test and update                | Coordinate scenario-based plan testing and update after material changes or exercises.                                                                              | Exercise record and plan revision log   |\n\n## SOP 3-09 \\| Inclusive risk assessment and ecosystem monitoring interface\n\nProcess owner: CDRRMO coordinates; sector offices and CENRO/DENR retain technical ownership. Purpose: Embed inclusion and protective ecosystem evidence in risk intelligence and preparedness.\n\n| **Step / control point**                      | **Required action and decision rule**                                                                                                        | **Mandatory record / output**            |\n|-----------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|\n| Set participation and data protocol           | Identify relevant sector groups and ecosystem custodians; define accessible consultation, lawful data use and consent where applicable.      | Stakeholder and data protocol            |\n| Collect disaggregated evidence                | Use official and lawfully held datasets; minimize personal data; document missing groups and coverage limitations.                           | Disaggregated dataset and privacy review |\n| Validate lived experience and access barriers | Consult women, PWD organizations, older persons, youth/children through appropriate safeguards, IP representatives and affected barangays.   | Consultation record and issue log        |\n| Assess ecosystem services                     | CENRO/DENR and relevant offices identify watershed, upland forest, mangrove and coastal assets, condition indicators and monitoring method.  | Ecosystem asset and indicator register   |\n| Integrate findings                            | Translate barriers and ecosystem findings into CDRA impact chains, MDA interventions, contingency plans and service continuity arrangements. | Updated CDRA/plan crosswalk              |\n| Monitor and report                            | Review inclusion and ecosystem indicators at approved intervals; record trend, uncertainty and corrective action.                            | Annual monitoring report                 |\n\n# 3.18 Responsibility assignment (RACI)\n\nRACI clarifies work allocation; it does not transfer statutory or regulatory authority. The matrix below is a proposed operational assignment and must be validated by office heads and the City Legal Office.\n\n| **Activity**                                  | **CDRRMO**      | **CPDO/LDC**                 | **Engineering/OBO/Zoning** | **Sector offices / utilities**     | **Barangays**               | **CLO / Mayor / CDRRMC**          |\n|-----------------------------------------------|-----------------|------------------------------|----------------------------|------------------------------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------------|\n| Maintain risk information register            | A/R             | C                            | C                          | R/C                                | C                           | I                                 |\n| Acquire and validate hazard source data       | R               | C                            | C                          | A/R for own technical data         | C                           | I                                 |\n| Prepare CDRA                                  | R/C             | A                            | C                          | R                                  | C                           | I                                 |\n| Approve/use CDRA in CLUP/CDP                  | C               | A/R                          | C                          | C                                  | C                           | Statutory approval route          |\n| Apply risk screening to investment proposals  | C               | A/R through planning process | R/C technical review       | R proposal owner                   | C                           | Budget/LFC/approving body         |\n| Prepare contingency plans                     | A/R coordinator | C                            | R/C                        | R hazard/sector lead               | R local plan/action         | Approval per local issuance       |\n| Prepare PSCP                                  | R/C coordinator | C                            | C                          | A/R by department for own services | I/C where service interface | Mayor/City Administrator approval |\n| Prepare and approve BDRRMP                    | C/support       | I/C                          | I                          | C                                  | A/R under applicable law    | DILG technical guidance           |\n| Monitor ecosystem risk-reduction assets       | C               | C                            | I                          | A/R CENRO/DENR                     | C                           | I                                 |\n| Review plan performance and corrective action | R/consolidates  | R for planning integration   | R for regulatory actions   | R for own actions                  | R for own plans             | CDRRMC oversight                  |\n\n# 3.19 Performance monitoring, records and corrective action\n\n| **Performance area**   | **Indicator / evidence**                                                                                                               | **Owner / review**                                           |\n|------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Risk data currency     | Share of priority datasets with complete metadata, current source version and assigned custodian; version register                     | CDRRMO GIS/data custodian; quarterly review                  |\n| Hazard map quality     | Priority hazard maps with signed validation, stated scale/use and publication metadata                                                 | CDRRMO and relevant technical office; annual/material update |\n| CDRA completeness      | Approved CDRA package; hazard coverage; exposure database completeness; MDAs; technical validation; correction of Naval/Biliran labels | CPDO/CDRA TWG; at each update                                |\n| Planning mainstreaming | CDRA MDAs cross-referenced to CLUP/CDP/LCCAP/LDIP/AIP and documented zoning/investment decisions                                       | CPDO/LDC; annual planning cycle                              |\n| Contingency readiness  | Priority hazards with approved plans, current contact/resource annexes and exercise/corrective-action records                          | CDRRMO and hazard leads; annual/exercise cycle               |\n| Service continuity     | Departments with completed essential-function inventories, BIAs, continuity strategies and tested plans                                | Continuity executive; annual review                          |\n| Barangay planning      | Verified barangay denominator; plans received, quality-reviewed, approved, tested and updated                                          | CDRRMO / DILG; quarterly status                              |\n| Inclusion              | Plans with documented consultation and accessible arrangements; unresolved barriers and corrective actions                             | Sector offices / CDRRMO; annual and post-event               |\n| Ecosystem assets       | Priority ecosystem assets with baseline condition, indicator, monitoring report and action owner                                       | CENRO/DENR; annual monitoring                                |\n\nThe CDRRMO shall consolidate evidence but shall not certify another office''s technical or statutory performance without that office''s validation. Each corrective action shall identify the finding, root cause, action, accountable owner, due date, resource implication, verification evidence and closure decision. Repeated non-closure shall be escalated through the CDRRMC and the appropriate executive or planning mechanism.\n\n# 3.20 Priority implementation and validation agenda\n\n| **Priority**                        | **Required action**                                                                                                                                                                       | **Lead / supporting offices**                                     | **Evidence of completion**                                   |\n|-------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|\n| P1 – Data and methodology integrity | Obtain signed DHSUD MC 2025-004 and current CDRA tools; secure current OCD contingency and PSCP manuals; reconcile IAT version metadata and workbook score/status conflicts               | CDRRMO; CPDO; CLO; OCD VIII; DHSUD as needed                      | Controlled source register; resolved version and scoring log |\n| P1 – CDRA completion                | Correct Naval/Biliran labels; validate existing flood/landslide/storm-surge data; scope and complete earthquake, liquefaction, tsunami and other priority hazard assessments as justified | CPDO/CDRA TWG; CDRRMO; PHIVOLCS; MGB; PAGASA; sector offices      | Validated CDRA package and MDA register                      |\n| P1 – Barangay denominator           | Resolve 85 versus 110 using official master list and document geographic coverage                                                                                                         | CPDO; City Secretary; DILG City LGOO                              | Signed barangay master list                                  |\n| P1 – PSCP                           | Designate continuity executive; inventory essential functions; complete BIA and dependencies; prepare, approve and test PSCP                                                              | Mayor/City Administrator; all departments; HRMO; ICT; CDRRMO      | Approved PSCP, BIA and exercise report                       |\n| P1 – Contingency plans              | Confirm priority hazard scenarios; complete missing hazard-specific plans; exercise and close corrective actions                                                                          | CDRRMO; hazard technical agencies; IMT/sector leads; barangays    | Approved plans and exercise records                          |\n| P2 – Planning mainstreaming         | Create CDRA-to-CLUP/CDP crosswalk and DRR/CCA investment screening process                                                                                                                | CPDO/LDC; Budget/LFC; Engineering/OBO/Zoning; CDRRMO              | Adopted screening form and documented planning minutes       |\n| P2 – Critical dependencies          | Convert existing facility/lifeline inventory into validated dependency maps and failure-chain scenarios                                                                                   | Asset owners; utilities; CPDO; CDRRMO                             | Validated dependency register and priority actions           |\n| P2 – Inclusion and ecosystems       | Hold documented sector validation; establish ecosystem baseline/indicators and annual monitoring                                                                                          | CSWDO; GAD; PWD/older-person/youth/IP representatives; CENRO/DENR | Consultation evidence and monitoring register                |\n\n# 3.21 Conclusion\n\nChapter 3 establishes a risk-intelligence and preparedness system that connects authoritative hazard information, method-compliant CDRA, exposure and vulnerability analysis, critical infrastructure dependencies, development planning, contingency planning, service continuity and barangay preparedness. Its central control is traceability: every important risk finding must have a source, a technical owner, a decision use, a responsible office and a record showing whether the required action was completed.\n\nThe chapter is not considered implemented merely because it is approved or included in the MOO. Institutionalization requires current datasets, signed technical validation, adopted plans, completed screening records, exercised contingency and continuity arrangements, verified barangay coverage, documented corrective actions and periodic evidence-based review.\n\n# REFERENCES\n\n- Department of Human Settlements and Urban Development. (2025). Memorandum Circular No. 2025-004: Enhanced Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment (CDRA) Policy. DHSUD.\n\n- Department of Human Settlements and Urban Development. (n.d.). Climate and Disaster Risk Assessment: The 5-Step+ CDRA process. DHSUD Learning Development Repository. https://sites.google.com/dhsud.gov.ph/cdra/introduction-to-cdra/5-steps-cdra-process\n\n- Department of the Interior and Local Government. (2025). Memorandum Circular No. 2025-035: Listo si KAP. DILG.\n\n- Republic Act No. 7160. (1991). Local Government Code of 1991.\n\n- Republic Act No. 9710. (2009). Magna Carta of Women.\n\n- Republic Act No. 9729. (2009). Climate Change Act of 2009, as amended by Republic Act No. 10174.\n\n- Republic Act No. 10121. (2010). Philippine Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Act of 2010. https://www.lawphil.net/statutes/repacts/ra2010/ra_10121_2010.html\n\n- Republic Act No. 10173. (2012). Data Privacy Act of 2012.\n\n- Republic Act No. 10821. (2016). Children''s Emergency Relief and Protection Act.\n\n- Republic Act No. 12076. (2024). Ligtas Pinoy Centers Act.\n\n- United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2015). Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction 2015–2030.\n\n- United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2017). Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities: Detailed Level Assessment.\n\nProject source documents used: Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC MOO Chapter 1 Consolidated (October 2026 working draft); Chapter 2 Enhanced Integrated Draft; Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC Institutional Assessment Tool v1.3 workbook (nine sheets). The workbook''s Guide/Crosswalk version references v1.1 in places; the instrument version history requires reconciliation before the assessment is treated as a controlled baseline.\n\nSource limitations: The full Ormoc CDRA report, Complete CLUP PDF, signed LDRRMP, current OCD contingency-planning manual, and PSCP Guidebook were not present among the files retrieved for this rebuild. Findings attributed to these documents are therefore limited to what is explicitly recorded in the IAT and Chapter 1 project materials. The City must attach and verify the original source documents before final technical adoption.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_v0.9.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft')
insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('ca63d006-3e83-5059-b840-b64fdd0642dd'::uuid,'b76900a1-d745-5b8a-a3bd-09b1b25792f2'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'# ANNEX 3-A \\| Indicator-to-process traceability matrix\n\n| **IAT ref** | **Requirement**                                           | **Chapter process**                              | **Process owner / interface**                | **Evidence required**                                                        |\n|-------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A3.1        | LDRRMP current, approved and aligned                      | LDRRMP review and mainstreaming (3-05)           | CDRRMO / CDRRMC; CPDO interface              | Approved plan, resolution, alignment crosswalk, annual review                |\n| A3.2        | DRR-CCA mainstreamed in CLUP/CDP/ELA/LCCAP and investment | CDRA Step 7; investment screening (3-05)         | CPDO/LDC; CDRRMO technical input             | CDRA-to-plan crosswalk, zoning/plan decisions, screening records             |\n| A3.3        | Hazard-specific contingency plans and testing             | Contingency plan development (3-06)              | CDRRMO coordinator; hazard leads             | Approved plans, exercise reports, corrective-action register                 |\n| A3.4        | PSCP covers CDRRMO and essential City services            | PSCP (3-07)                                      | Continuity executive; department heads       | Essential functions inventory, BIA, PSCP, exercise                           |\n| A3.7        | Barangay DRRM plans reviewed/consolidated                 | Barangay QA (3-08)                               | BDRRMC/BDC; CDRRMO and DILG review           | Verified master list, official QA tool, status matrix                        |\n| A5.1        | Updated multi-hazard maps at barangay scale               | Hazard mapping (3-01); repository (3-02)         | CDRRMO GIS; source agencies                  | Validated map package, metadata and version register                         |\n| A5.2        | CDRA with exposure and vulnerability                      | CDRA (3-03); impact-chain analysis               | CPDO/CDRA TWG; CDRRMO                        | CDRA report, geodatabase, risk tables, MDAs                                  |\n| A5.3        | Disaggregated at-risk population database                 | CDRA exposure database; inclusion (3-09)         | CBMS/data custodian; CSWDO/Health; barangays | Current lawful dataset, data dictionary, privacy review                      |\n| A5.8        | Critical facilities and dependency/failure-chain map      | Critical facilities (3-04)                       | Asset owners/utilities; CDRRMO coordinates   | Asset register, dependency diagram, failure-chain sheets                     |\n| A7.6        | Inclusive DRRM embedded in plans and services             | Inclusive assessment (3-09); all plan SOPs       | Sector offices; CDRRMO; representatives      | Accessible consultation records, plan provisions, barrier closure            |\n| C1.1        | Risk scenarios inform CDP/CLUP and investments            | CDRA MDA and planning interface (3-03/3-05)      | CPDO/LDC                                     | Scenario register and plan/investment crosswalk                              |\n| C1.2        | DRR/CCA screening in project and budget approval          | Investment screening (3-05)                      | CPDO; Budget/LFC; technical regulators       | Screening form, LDC/LFC minutes, appraisal record                            |\n| C1.3        | Loss analysis beyond physical assets                      | Exposure/loss analysis (3-03)                    | CPDO and sector data owners                  | Livelihood, jobs, displacement, agriculture, ecosystem and heritage analysis |\n| C1.4        | Risk-sensitive zoning and permit guidelines               | Hazard map use (3-01); planning interface (3-05) | CPDO/Zoning; Engineering/OBO                 | Adopted overlays/guidelines and sample permit review                         |\n| C3.1        | Protective ecosystems identified and monitored            | Inclusive/ecosystem interface (3-09)             | CENRO/DENR; City Agriculture                 | Ecosystem register, baseline and annual monitoring report                    |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_Annex_3-A.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft'),
('ec5c8b77-3dd9-5787-a89f-b3b9d0cf117d'::uuid,'e0e7eae4-41d7-57cf-a334-3a71d73c4917'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'# ANNEX 3-B \\| Form 3-01 – Risk information and map request\n\n| **Field**               | **Required entry**                                               | **Field**                        | **Required entry**                                   |\n|-------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------|\n| Request ID / date       | Unique ID and date received                                      | Requesting office / focal person | Name, designation and contact                        |\n| Decision purpose        | Screening / public information / CLUP / technical review / other | Hazard / geography               | Hazard, barangay(s), coordinates or area             |\n| Required scale / format | Scale, map format, GIS format and output                         | Required-by date                 | Date and reason                                      |\n| Source product          | Agency, product title, version/date, URL/reference               | Limitations                      | Scale, uncertainty, no-data, restrictions            |\n| Validation office       | Technical reviewer and review date                               | Approval/use category            | Internal / public / planning / regulatory            |\n| Release details         | Version, date, distribution and custodian                        | Revision trigger                 | Source update / event / boundary change / correction |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_Annex_3-B.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft'),
('affcbd9c-532f-5224-92f7-2050290c040e'::uuid,'9f3a52ba-ab8e-531a-ad9a-84833f0a6ea5'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'# ANNEX 3-C \\| Form 3-02 – Dataset metadata and quality checklist\n\n| **Metadata / quality field**                   | **Check / entry**                                     |\n|------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|\n| Dataset ID, title and description              | Record unique ID, purpose and content                 |\n| Source agency and original product reference   | Record issuing authority and original file            |\n| Data date, acquisition date and update cycle   | Distinguish source date from download date            |\n| Geographic coverage and scale/resolution       | Record City/barangay coverage and suitability         |\n| Coordinate reference system and units          | Record original CRS and any transformation            |\n| Method, scenario, baseline and time horizon    | Record model/method assumptions and projection period |\n| Attributes, legend and classification          | Check classes, units, code lists and interpretation   |\n| Completeness, geometry and no-data areas       | Record defects and treatment                          |\n| Limitations and uncertainty                    | Record known constraints and prohibited uses          |\n| Custodian, access classification and retention | Record responsible office and access controls         |\n| Technical validation and approval              | Reviewer, date, comments and approved use             |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_Annex_3-C.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft'),
('af853e65-1d27-5c0a-822e-e0a7602418a1'::uuid,'e7b3e024-7d39-52cf-a4ee-55550a7c16b8'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'# ANNEX 3-D \\| Form 3-03 – CDRA hazard-impact scoping matrix\n\n| **Hazard / climate stressor** | **Source / scenario / horizon** | **Affected sectors / elements**                          | **Impact chain and spatial area**           | **Data gap / uncertainty**    | **Validation owner** |\n|-------------------------------|---------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|-------------------------------|----------------------|\n| \\[Enter hazard\\]              | \\[Source, date, scenario\\]      | \\[People, buildings, livelihoods, services, ecosystems\\] | \\[Initiating event → impact → consequence\\] | \\[Missing data / confidence\\] | \\[Office / agency\\]  |\n| \\[Enter hazard\\]              | \\[Source, date, scenario\\]      | \\[People, buildings, livelihoods, services, ecosystems\\] | \\[Initiating event → impact → consequence\\] | \\[Missing data / confidence\\] | \\[Office / agency\\]  |\n\nInstructions: Complete one row per hazard-sector pathway. Identify assumptions and data gaps explicitly. Do not assign risk classes outside the current DHSUD-prescribed method.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_Annex_3-D.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft'),
('dbc34bcb-980b-567b-946d-eaa30c21da36'::uuid,'90c1dec6-06ac-5832-a1f9-8b356a61d78e'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'# ANNEX 3-E \\| Form 3-04 – CDRA Major Decision Area register\n\n| **MDA ID / location**            | **Evidence and risk finding**     | **Affected people/assets / system** | **Policy or investment decision required**                                              | **Lead office / partners** | **Indicator / target / review date** |\n|----------------------------------|-----------------------------------|-------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------|--------------------------------------|\n| \\[ID / barangay / spatial unit\\] | \\[CDRA result and map reference\\] | \\[Disaggregated groups and assets\\] | \\[Land-use, infrastructure, social protection, ecosystem or preparedness intervention\\] | \\[Owner\\]                  | \\[Measure and due date\\]             |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_Annex_3-E.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft'),
('496fc1b9-dd7a-5293-910f-300c48c85d8d'::uuid,'46a8c9fd-c613-58c1-9879-078061eecd35'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'# ANNEX 3-F \\| Form 3-05 – Critical facilities and dependency register\n\n| **Asset ID / name** | **Owner / operator** | **Service / capacity / users**       | **Hazard exposure / vulnerability** | **Dependencies / downstream services**            | **Backup / redundancy / restoration**        | **Validation date**  |\n|---------------------|----------------------|--------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|----------------------|\n| \\[ID\\]              | \\[Office / utility\\] | \\[Function, capacity, service area\\] | \\[Map/source and condition\\]        | \\[Power, water, telecom, road, fuel, staff, ICT\\] | \\[Alternate, backup, estimated restoration\\] | \\[Date / signatory\\] |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_Annex_3-F.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft'),
('310c2a3a-25c2-5fbe-be01-6c09904316eb'::uuid,'9408a317-5f2d-5396-bfd1-9eaad44254d6'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'# ANNEX 3-G \\| Form 3-06 – Contingency plan quality and exercise record\n\n| **Control area**                     | **Required evidence / reviewer finding**                                         | **Status / corrective action** |\n|--------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n| Scenario and assumptions             | Source, magnitude, location, lead time, cascading effects and uncertainty        |                                |\n| Objectives and concept of operations | Priorities, phases, EOC/ICS interface and transition                             |                                |\n| Roles and tasking                    | Lead/support agencies, barangay roles, escalation and alternates                 |                                |\n| Triggers and warning                 | Authoritative source, action threshold, decision authority and dissemination     |                                |\n| Operations and protection            | Evacuation, shelter, health, logistics, protection, MHPSS and public information |                                |\n| Resources and logistics              | Personnel, equipment, supplies, transport, facilities and gaps                   |                                |\n| Information management               | Situation reporting, validation, decision log and records                        |                                |\n| Exercise results                     | Objectives, observations, decisions, performance and lessons                     |                                |\n| Corrective actions                   | Action, owner, deadline, resource and closure evidence                           |                                |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_Annex_3-G.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft'),
('c2879e61-35be-52e1-afac-5d5156122acc'::uuid,'84f5dd93-d2d8-5de7-b21a-e2ff7911517c'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'# ANNEX 3-H \\| Form 3-07 – Essential functions inventory and BIA\n\n| **Department / service** | **Essential function / legal basis** | **Service users / minimum level**          | **People / skills / succession** | **Facilities / ICT / records** | **Dependencies / suppliers**                 | **Impact over time / priority**                        | **Recovery strategy / owner**  |\n|--------------------------|--------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n| \\[Office\\]               | \\[Function and authority\\]           | \\[Who depends on service; minimum output\\] | \\[Key posts, alternates\\]        | \\[Site, system, records\\]      | \\[Power, water, telecom, transport, vendor\\] | \\[Life safety, legal, welfare, financial; time bands\\] | \\[Strategy, owner, resources\\] |\n\nInstructions: Department head validates each function. Proposed recovery time and minimum service levels must be justified by BIA evidence and approved by the designated continuity executive; do not use unsupported generic time targets.\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_Annex_3-H.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft'),
('89ffbd8b-1c3f-5560-b009-bf04d85908de'::uuid,'ae91e295-4eee-5511-965a-8a53ea3b2b19'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'# ANNEX 3-I \\| Form 3-08 – Barangay DRRM plan review status\n\n| **Barangay official name / ID** | **Master-list source** | **Plan period / version** | **Approval evidence** | **Quality tool / review date** | **Status** | **Required corrections / due date** | **Exercise / update evidence** |\n|---------------------------------|------------------------|---------------------------|-----------------------|--------------------------------|------------|-------------------------------------|--------------------------------|\n| \\[Barangay\\]                    | \\[Official source\\]    | \\[Period/version\\]        | \\[Resolution/date\\]   | \\[Tool/version/date\\]          | \\[Status\\] | \\[Action and due date\\]             | \\[Record\\]                     |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_Annex_3-I.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft'),
('04374658-776b-58e0-a5da-cbaf3d491113'::uuid,'62fd7931-d11e-5a0f-b471-804bba6800ea'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'# ANNEX 3-J \\| Form 3-09 – DRR/CCA project screening\n\n| **Project / proponent / site** | **Hazard and climate exposure** | **People/assets affected / inclusion** | **Siting/design/ecosystem implications**    | **Risk treatment / residual risk** | **Technical reviewers**               | **Planning/budget decision**           |\n|--------------------------------|---------------------------------|----------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------|------------------------------------|---------------------------------------|----------------------------------------|\n| \\[Project\\]                    | \\[Map/source/date/scenario\\]    | \\[Groups and service impacts\\]         | \\[Alternatives, codes, ecosystem/heritage\\] | \\[Measures, cost, residual risk\\]  | \\[CPDO/Engineering/OBO/CENRO/CDRRMO\\] | \\[LDC/LFC/Budget/approving authority\\] |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_Annex_3-J.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft'),
('64108816-c4f5-5ea3-8478-62c41d436c6a'::uuid,'26c123c7-cb03-5d9a-8dce-52900f5e108d'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'# ANNEX 3-K \\| Form 3-10 – Consultation and inclusion validation record\n\n| **Consulted group / organization** | **Date / accessible method**       | **Risk or access issue raised** | **Evidence / location** | **Plan or design response**          | **Responsible office / closure** |\n|------------------------------------|------------------------------------|---------------------------------|-------------------------|--------------------------------------|----------------------------------|\n| \\[Group\\]                          | \\[Date, language, access support\\] | \\[Issue\\]                       | \\[Evidence\\]            | \\[Response / reason if not adopted\\] | \\[Owner / due date\\]             |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_Annex_3-K.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft'),
('0e7bf630-a221-5a38-b51e-7df22356db41'::uuid,'d323303a-0f9e-5c64-93d6-de5b941aebca'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'# ANNEX 3-L \\| Form 3-11 – Corrective action and document revision register\n\n| **Finding / source**               | **Root cause** | **Corrective action** | **Accountable owner** | **Due date / resources** | **Verification evidence** | **Closure / escalation**     |\n|------------------------------------|----------------|-----------------------|-----------------------|--------------------------|---------------------------|------------------------------|\n| \\[IAT / exercise / event / audit\\] | \\[Cause\\]      | \\[Action\\]            | \\[Office / official\\] | \\[Date / budget\\]        | \\[Record / test\\]         | \\[Closed by / escalated to\\] |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_Annex_3-L.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft'),
('cc9f6cce-273c-5adc-8a6e-49fe0311bce9'::uuid,'1e6d98b2-b29d-52c9-9f6d-537cf7b2e265'::uuid,'v0.9 working draft',E'# ANNEX 3-M \\| Source and methodology register\n\n| **Source**                                                                        | **Verified use in this chapter**                                                                                          | **Status / qualification**                                                                                                                     |\n|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Republic Act No. 10121 (2010), Lawphil / Supreme Court E-Library                  | Statutory LDRRMC and LDRRMO functions; risk assessment, risk information, local risk map, LDRRMP and contingency planning | Official statutory text; apply with current IRR                                                                                                |\n| DHSUD Memorandum Circular No. 2025-004, Enhanced CDRA Policy                      | Policy intent and minimum approach for standardized enhanced CDRA                                                         | DHSUD learning repository confirms policy purpose; obtain signed circular and annexes for exact operative requirements                         |\n| DHSUD CDRA learning repository: 5-Step+ CDRA Process                              | Detailed sequence for CDRA steps and subsequent compilation, CLUP mainstreaming, M&E and communication                    | Official DHSUD training material; apply current technical parameters and prescribed tools                                                      |\n| PHIVOLCS GIS hazard-map download instructions and Hazard Assessment Services      | Source acquisition, product scale/availability caveats and limits of hazard assessment reports                            | Official PHIVOLCS pages; not a substitute for current technical manuals                                                                        |\n| DILG MC No. 2025-035 / Listo si KAP information                                   | Barangay preparedness and planning responsibilities                                                                       | DILG regional publication confirms initiative; use signed circular and current national quality tools                                          |\n| DILG Quality Assessment System / BDRRM plan template                              | Barangay plan review structure and quality assurance                                                                      | Use current official versions; legacy templates are not presumed current                                                                       |\n| OCD/NDRRMC PSCP Guidebook                                                         | Essential functions, BIA and continuity planning                                                                          | Listed in IAT References as project reference; exact edition and original file must be verified before final technical sign-off                |\n| OCD/NDRRMC contingency-planning manual                                            | Hazard scenario and response planning method                                                                              | Exact edition and original file not located in retrieved project materials; obtain from OCD Region VIII and reconcile this SOP before approval |\n| UNDRR Disaster Resilience Scorecard for Cities – Detailed Level Assessment (2017) | Good-practice benchmarks for scenarios, critical asset failure chains, risk-sensitive planning and ecosystem services     | Benchmark only; IAT crosswalk identifies its adapted indicators                                                                                |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/MOO_Ch3_Annex_3-M.md (Chapter 3, v0.9 working draft)','draft')
